  "A tail held up with a small hook or curl at the tip, like a question mark, is a relaxed and friendly signal.",
  "When a cat's tail quivers while held straight up, the cat is usually excited to see whoever is in front of it.",
  "A tail that lashes or thumps from side to side means a cat is irritated or overstimulated, not wagging with joy.",
  "A tail tucked low or wrapped tightly around the body signals a nervous or unhappy cat.",
  "The fur on a frightened cat's tail stands on end by reflex, puffing the tail out to make the cat look bigger.",
  "The Halloween cat pose, arched back, puffed fur and stiff legs, is a scared cat trying to look as large as it can.",
  "Forward-facing ears show a cat is interested and relaxed.",
  "Ears flattened to the sides and back, sometimes called airplane ears, mean a cat is scared, annoyed or ready to fight.",
  "Cats' ears swivel on their own. One ear turned back while the cat faces forward means it is tracking a sound behind it.",
  "The slow blink is friendly. A study found cats slow blink back at owners who do it and approach strangers who do it.",
  "A direct, unblinking stare is a challenge between cats, so a cat that looks away is being polite, not rude.",
  "A cat that rolls over and shows its belly is saying it feels safe, but it is not usually asking for a belly rub.",
  "The belly is a vulnerable spot, so many cats grab or kick a hand that touches it even after rolling over happily.",
  "Cats roll onto their side in front of a person or another cat as a friendly display sometimes called the social roll.",
  "Many cats bite gently after a long petting session. It is a sign they have had enough rather than an attack.",
  "A cat that suddenly starts grooming in the middle of a tense moment is probably using grooming to calm itself down.",
  "Many cats arch their back up into a stroking hand to press harder. This happy arch is unlike the stiff arch of fear.",
  "Whiskers pushed forward show a cat is curious or on the hunt. Whiskers pulled flat against the face show fear.",
  "A cat with a twitching tail tip while sitting still is usually concentrating hard on something, such as a bird outside.",
  "Kneading, pushing with alternate front paws on a soft blanket or a lap, is a kitten habit that once got milk flowing.",
  "Kneading often comes with purring and a dreamy expression, and some cats drool or suckle on fabric while they knead.",
  "Cats also knead to mark. Scent glands between the toes leave a smell on the blanket or lap being kneaded.",
  "The rhythmic pushing of kneading is often nicknamed making biscuits.",
  "Head bunting, a firm bump of the forehead against a person or another cat, is a greeting reserved for those it trusts.",
  "Cats rub their cheeks and chin on furniture, door frames and people, leaving scent that marks them as familiar and safe.",
  "A cat weaving between a person's legs and wrapping its tail around them is greeting and scent marking at the same time.",
  "Cats rub their cheeks on new objects brought into the home, adding their own scent to anything that smells strange.",
  "Many cats sniff their owner's shoes and bag when they get home, checking the scents of where they have been.",
  "A cat back from the vet is sometimes hissed at by its housemates. It smells wrong until the clinic scent wears off.",
  "Spraying is different from ordinary urination. The cat stands, lifts a quivering tail and squirts a little onto a wall.",
  "Both male and female cats can spray, though unneutered males do it most.",
  "Neutering stops or greatly reduces spraying in most male cats.",
  "Confident cats sometimes leave droppings unburied in a visible spot as a territorial message.",
  "Cats scratch to shed worn claw sheaths, to stretch their back and shoulders, and to leave scent and visual marks.",
  "Scratched spots work like a message board. Cats pick visible places, often near doorways or where they sleep.",
  "A cat often has a good scratch and stretch right after waking up.",
  "Cats prefer a scratching post tall enough to stretch fully against and sturdy enough not to wobble.",
  "Scratching the sofa in front of an owner who has just come home can be a cat's way of showing excitement.",
  "Cats are stalk and ambush hunters. They creep low, freeze, then pounce rather than chase prey over a long distance.",
  "The rear-end wiggle before a pounce probably helps a cat set its back feet for a strong push-off.",
  "A cat's killing bite is aimed at the back of the prey's neck, where the teeth slip between the bones of the spine.",
  "Cats hunt even when well fed. Hunger and hunting are driven by separate systems, so a full cat still stalks.",
  "Mice and voles move about most at dawn and dusk, so that is when cats do most of their hunting.",
  "A hunting cat may sit still for a long time beside a mouse hole, waiting for the mouse to come out.",
  "Cats often toy with prey they have caught, batting it about, which may be a way of tiring it before a safe bite.",
  "Cats often carry prey home to eat in a safe place. A pet cat may then abandon it because it is not hungry.",
  "A study that put cameras on pet cats found they brought home less than a quarter of what they caught.",
  "Studies of what pet cats bring home found a few cats catch most of the prey, while many catch very little.",
  "Cats stalk small moving things. A leaf, a shadow or a fly can set off the whole hunting sequence.",
  "Adult cat play copies the stalk, pounce and bite of a hunt, which is why a toy on a string gets such a reaction.",
  "A toy that moves away from a cat is more exciting than one coming toward it, because prey runs away.",
  "Cats play hardest with toys about the size of a mouse or a small bird, the prey they are built to catch.",
  "A study found hungry cats play more intensely with toys, treating them more like real prey.",
  "Cats quickly tire of a toy they have seen a lot. A study found they play more when the toys are swapped around.",
  "Many cats carry a favourite toy around while making a special yowl, just as they would with prey.",
  "A laser dot that can never be caught can leave a cat frustrated, which is why many trainers end play with a real toy.",
  "Cats pounce on sheets being shaken onto a bed because the moving fabric and the hidden hands look like prey.",
  "Sudden bursts of racing around the house, often in the evening, are pent-up energy in an animal built for short hunts.",
  "Many cats race about right after using the litter box. The reason is not known for certain.",
  "Cats are naturally most active around dawn and dusk, which is why many tear around the house early in the morning.",
  "Cats that get a good play session in the evening tend to settle better overnight.",
  "Some cats scratch at the floor around a food bowl as if to bury leftovers, a habit linked to hiding food from rivals.",
  "Cats bury their waste partly to hide their scent from larger predators and from rival cats.",
  "The habit of burying waste in loose material is why most cats take to a litter tray with no training at all.",
  "Cats often sniff and scratch at the litter before and after using it, part of a natural burying routine.",
  "A cat wound up by something it cannot reach, such as a cat outside the window, may turn on a nearby housemate or person.",
  "An indoor cat treats the house as its territory and may get upset by a strange cat seen through the window.",
  "Outdoor cats often patrol the same route each day, renewing scent marks along the way.",
  "Some cats try to return to their old home after a move. The pull of familiar territory is strong.",
  "Adult cats sleep around 12 to 16 hours a day, more than most other mammals.",
  "Much of a cat's sleep is light dozing. The ears keep moving and the cat can be fully awake in a moment.",
  "Cats also have deep sleep with rapid eye movement (REM), during which paws, whiskers and tails may twitch.",
  "Research in the 1960s found that cats whose sleep paralysis was switched off would stalk and pounce while asleep.",
  "Cats sleep in many short stretches through the day and night rather than in one long block.",
  "A cat resting in a loaf, paws tucked under the body, can be up and running in an instant. It is resting, not asleep.",
  "A cat curled in a tight ball with its tail over its nose is holding in warmth and protecting its belly.",
  "A cat sleeping with a paw over its face may be shielding its eyes from light or keeping its nose warm.",
  "A cat that sleeps stretched out on its back with its belly showing feels completely safe in that place.",
  "Cats curl up tightly when cold and sprawl out flat when warm, so sleeping posture is a rough thermometer.",
  "Cats sleep on their people for warmth, security and because the person smells familiar.",
  "Pet cats shift their sleep to fit their owner's routine, dozing through the workday and waking as the household stirs.",
  "Cats tend to sleep more on cold, wet days, as they would when hunting conditions are poor.",
  "Cats often rotate between several favourite sleeping spots, switching now and then for reasons not fully understood.",
  "A basket of warm laundry draws cats for its heat and the familiar smell of the household.",
  "In hot weather some cats sleep in a sink or on tiles because the cool surface draws heat out of them.",
  "Cats spend a big share of their waking time grooming, often estimated at between a third and a half.",
  "Grooming also spreads oils through the coat, cools the cat as saliva evaporates and removes the smell of a meal.",
  "Cats usually wash in a set order, starting with the face using a licked paw and then moving back along the body.",
  "Cats that live together groom each other's heads and necks, places hard to reach alone. This is called allogrooming.",
  "Allogrooming happens mostly between friendly cats, and a study found the more confident cat does most of the grooming.",
  "A cat licking its owner is treating them like a member of its group. It is a social grooming gesture.",
  "Many cats wash right after being stroked, probably to replace the person's scent with their own.",
  "Cats love boxes because an enclosed space feels safe. A shelter study found cats given boxes got over stress faster.",
  "Cats squeeze into tight spaces partly to feel safe and partly because a snug spot keeps them warm.",
  "A box has walls on most sides, so a cat inside can watch the one open side instead of the whole room.",
  "Cats sit in squares taped on the floor. A study found they even sit in an illusion of a square made from broken lines.",
  "Cats like to sit on a mat, a sheet of paper or any clearly bordered patch on the floor.",
  "Cats like high places because height gives a view of threats and prey and keeps them out of reach of other animals.",
  "In a home with several cats, the cat sitting highest is often the most confident one.",
  "The top of a fridge or wardrobe ticks two boxes for a cat: it is high up and it is often warm.",
  "A frightened cat heads for a hiding place, often under a bed or behind a sofa, and may stay there for hours.",
  "Cats seek out sunny spots, radiators and warm laptops. They are comfortable at temperatures that feel too hot to people.",
  "A cat sitting on a laptop or keyboard also gets attention, and many cats learn that quickly.",
  "Many cats prefer running water from a tap to a still bowl. Moving water may seem fresher and is easier to see.",
  "Some cats paddle at their water bowl before drinking. It may help them judge where the surface is.",
  "Many cats prefer to drink some distance away from their food bowl.",
  "Most cats dislike being soaked because wet fur is heavy, cold and slow to dry, yet many love to watch dripping water.",
  "A cat that steps in water usually shakes each paw in turn. Cats dislike wet feet almost as much as wet fur.",
  "About two thirds of cats respond to catnip. They roll, rub, drool and leap about for a few minutes, then lose interest.",
  "The response to catnip is inherited, and a cat that does not react will not learn to.",
  "After a catnip session a cat is usually immune to it for an hour or more before it can respond again.",
  "Silver vine, a plant from Asia, sets off a catnip-like response in many cats that ignore catnip.",
  "A study found that rubbing on catnip and silver vine leaves a chemical on the fur that repels mosquitoes.",
  "Cats dislike change. Moved furniture or a new smell in the home can unsettle a cat for days.",
  "Many cats hate a closed door and meow or paw to get through. The door cuts them off from part of their territory.",
  "Cats remember places where something bad happened and may avoid the carrier or a room for a long time.",
  "Cats have a strong sense of routine and may wake their owner at the same time each morning for breakfast.",
  "Cats that want breakfast learn to pat a sleeping face or knock things over. Whatever worked once gets repeated.",
  "Many cats wait by the door at the usual homecoming time. They learn the daily routine and the sound of familiar steps.",
  "Cats hunt alone, but they are not strictly solitary. Where food is plentiful, feral cats live in colonies.",
  "Feral cat colonies are built around related females, who share nursing duties and defend the kittens together.",
  "Male feral cats are loosely attached to colonies and often move between several of them.",
  "Even cats living in a colony go off to hunt by themselves. Cats do not hunt in packs.",
  "A cat's territory size depends on food. A well-fed pet cat may use a few gardens, while a farm cat ranges much wider.",
  "GPS studies of pet cats found most stay within a few hundred metres of home, though a few wander much further.",
  "Cats time-share space. Two cats that avoid each other may use the same path at different times of day.",
  "Cats greeting one another touch noses first and then sniff each other's flanks and rear.",
  "In cat colonies, the raised tail is the signal that one cat wants to approach another in a friendly way.",
  "A cat presenting its rear to a person is offering the same greeting it gives another cat: an invitation to sniff.",
  "Cats that live together and get on will rub flanks, groom each other and sleep touching, the signs of a bonded pair.",
  "Groups of cats have no strict rank order. Relationships are worked out cat by cat and can change with time.",
  "Cats in the same home often avoid eye contact and give each other space. That is peaceful coexistence, not dislike.",
  "A study of cats and dogs sharing a home found most got on well, especially when the cat came first and both were young.",
  "Dogs and cats can misread each other. A wagging tail is friendly in a dog but a sign of irritation in a cat.",
  "A cat that head bunts or rubs on a dog is treating the dog as part of its social group.",
  "A study found cats can pick out their own name from similar-sounding words, even when a stranger says it.",
  "A study found cats living together learn each other's names, staring longer when a name went with the wrong cat's face.",
  "Cats know their owner's voice. A study found they turn their ears and head toward it but rarely bother to come.",
  "A study found cats track where their owner is by voice and act surprised if the voice seems to jump to another room.",
  "A study found cats react when their owner talks to them in a special high voice, but not when a stranger does the same.",
  "A study found cats can follow a person's pointing finger to find hidden food, a skill once thought special to dogs.",
  "In one study, most cats chose human company over food, toys or an interesting smell when offered all four.",
  "Cats form attachments to their owners much as babies and dogs do. A study found about two thirds were securely attached.",
  "A study found cats glance between a strange object and their owner's face, checking how the owner reacts to it.",
  "A study found cats were more likely to purr and rub against an owner who was smiling than one who was frowning.",
  "A study found cats spend more time with people who wait for the cat to approach than with those who reach out first.",
  "Most cats like being stroked around the head and cheeks, where scent glands sit, and many dislike the base of the tail.",
  "A study found cats pay more attention to music composed with cat-like tempos and pitches than to music made for people.",
  "The behaviourist John Bradshaw has suggested cats treat people much like big, friendly, slightly clumsy cats.",
  "A cat that sits just out of reach when called is not being rude. Cats prefer to control when contact happens.",
  "Many cats raise their rear when stroked at the base of the tail, a reflex linked to the friendly greeting posture.",
  "Cats knock objects off tables partly to test them with a paw and partly because it reliably gets a reaction.",
  "A cat that stares at its owner and then at a cupboard is often asking for the food kept there.",
  "Cats sometimes bring toys to their owners at night and meow. It is an invitation to play.",
  "Many cats watch birds and mice on TV, and some paw at the screen, though they soon learn the prey cannot be caught.",
  "Cats do not recognise themselves in mirrors. Most treat the reflection as another cat at first and then ignore it.",
  "Cats can learn a task faster by watching another cat do it first, experiments have shown.",
  "Some cats learn to open cupboards and doors by watching people, then pulling handles with their paws.",
  "Cats can learn tricks such as sit, high five and come when called, especially with food rewards and short sessions.",
  "Cats do not learn from punishment the way people expect. A cat shouted at usually just learns to fear the person.",
  "Cats respond to their name best when hearing it has often been followed by something good, such as food or play.",
  "A cat that turns away, tucks its paws in and half closes its eyes is settling down and wants to be left alone.",
  "A fast head-on approach makes most cats back away. They are more at ease with someone who comes in slowly from the side.",
  "A cat that sniffs an offered finger and rubs on it is accepting the greeting. One that sniffs and backs off is not.",
  "Cats stop and groom after a fall or a stumble. It looks like embarrassment but is a way of settling themselves.",
  "A cat lying across a doorway or hallway is often choosing a spot where it can keep track of everyone's movements.",
  "Cats sometimes leave toys in a water bowl or a bed. Behaviourists think they are stashing prey somewhere safe.",
  "Cats hold their tail low and straight out behind them while stalking, which helps balance and avoids drawing attention.",
  "Ambushing a passing ankle from behind a door is a hunting game. The cat is practising the pounce, not holding a grudge.",
  "A female cat in heat yowls loudly and rolls about, a display that brings tomcats in from a wide area.",
  "A cat that growls over its food bowl is guarding a resource, a habit common in cats that once went hungry.",
  "Mutual grooming between cats sometimes ends in a scuffle when one cat has had enough and the other misses the signal.",
  "Friendly cats rub heads and flanks together, a habit called allorubbing that mixes their scents into a group smell.",
  "Cats sniff the spray marks of other cats and often add their own mark on top, keeping a scent conversation going.",
  "A cat that starts spraying indoors usually feels insecure, often because of a new cat, a move or a strange cat outside.",
  "Cats scratch more when stressed or when a new cat is about, as the marks and the scent help them feel more secure.",
  "Some cats prefer to scratch flat surfaces such as rugs, others go for upright posts, and many like both.",
  "A cat's range has a core area, used daily for sleeping and eating, and a wider hunting range it visits less often.",
  "Studies of farm and feral cats found that male ranges are often several times larger than female ranges.",
  "Feral cats living close to people tend to be most active at night, when the streets are quiet.",
  "In a home with several cats, two may become close friends while a third keeps to itself. Cats pick their own friends.",
  "Two adult cats meeting for the first time often take weeks to accept each other, and some never get past being polite.",
  "In cold weather cats that get along often sleep in a heap, sharing warmth.",
  "Many cats dislike being picked up and carried, since being lifted off the ground takes away their ability to flee.",
  "A cat that takes the warm spot a person has just left is after the heat, though the familiar scent helps too.",
  "Many cats hide during thunderstorms and fireworks, choosing a dark enclosed spot and waiting until the noise stops.",
  "Cats stretch after almost every nap, which loosens muscles that stiffen during a long rest.",
  "A study found cats greeted their owner with more purring and stretching after four hours away than after half an hour.",
  "Cats roll in dusty soil or on a warm path. The roll may spread scent, scratch an itch or simply feel good.",
  "Pet cats catch most of their prey in spring and summer, when young birds and small mammals are plentiful.",
  "Studies of what pet cats bring home find small mammals come first, birds second, and reptiles and insects far behind.",
  "Some cats bring prey home alive and let it go indoors, which is how a mouse ends up behind the fridge.",
  "Unlike many animals, cats keep playing well into adulthood, batting objects about on their own when no one is around.",
  "Some cats play fetch, bringing a toy back to be thrown again. A study found most of them started the game themselves.",
  "Many cats spend hours at a window. Watching birds and passers-by gives an indoor cat much of the interest of a hunt.",
  "Valerian root and Tatarian honeysuckle wood set off the same rolling and rubbing as catnip in many cats.",
  "In a puzzle they cannot solve, dogs look to their owner for help far sooner than cats do, a study found.",
  "A study found cats follow a person's gaze to find hidden food, not only a pointing finger.",
  "A large study of pet cats found five broad personality traits, including friendliness, impulsiveness and nervousness.",
  "A study found cats explore a strange room more readily when their owner is present, much as toddlers do.",
  "A cat moved to a new home often hides for the first few days, coming out at night to eat and explore when all is quiet.",
  "Cats often pick a resting spot with their back to a wall, so nothing can approach them from behind.",
  "Cats that lick a hand and then nip it treat it as they would another cat while grooming. It is rarely aggression.",
  "Cats in a colony know their own members and often chase off a strange cat that tries to join them at the food.",
  "Rolling onto the back in a fight puts all four sets of claws in use. It is a fighting position, not a surrender.",
  "Some cats sleep with their eyes a little open, and the eyes can be seen flicking about under the lids in deep sleep.",
  "In a multi-cat home one cat may sit in the path to the litter tray or food bowl, a quiet way of bullying another.",
  "Cats sometimes freeze and stare at nothing. They are usually tracking a sound or a tiny movement a person cannot detect.",
  "Cats often sit and stare at a spot where they once saw a mouse, sometimes returning to watch it for days afterwards.",
  "Many cats enjoy being brushed because it feels like being groomed by another cat, especially around the head and neck.",
  "A cat crouched flat with ears back and eyes wide is frightened, and a frightened cat lashes out if it feels cornered.",
  "Cats that share a home often eat side by side without fuss, yet each still prefers its own bowl and spot.",
  "Cats hide when they are frightened rather than running far, so a lost indoor cat is often very close to home.",
  "Cats scratch at glass doors and windows to ask to be let out or in, having learned that the gesture brings a person.",
  "Unneutered tomcats spray along their routes, and the strong smell tells rivals and females who has been by.",
  "Cats shut out of a room will often rush in to sniff every corner when the door opens, checking what has changed.",
  "A cat with the tip of its tongue left sticking out has usually been distracted mid-wash and forgotten to pull it in.",
  "A cat sitting upright with its tail wrapped neatly around its front feet is alert and calm, keeping an eye on things.",
  "Cats quickly learn the sound of the treat bag or food tin and come running at it even from a sound sleep.",
}

-- Kittens, growing up, growing old, health and food (256)
add {
  "A cat's pregnancy lasts about nine weeks, usually between 63 and 67 days.",
  "A female cat is called a queen, and a typical litter has three to five kittens.",
  "Litters of just one kitten or of more than eight both happen, though three to five is usual.",
  "One litter can have more than one father, because a queen may mate with several males while in heat.",
  "Kittens in a single litter having different fathers is called superfecundation.",
  "A pregnant queen looks for a quiet, dark, hidden spot to give birth, such as a wardrobe or under a bed.",
  "A mother cat may carry her kittens one by one to a new hiding place if the first nest is disturbed.",
  "Kittens are born inside a thin membrane sac, which the mother usually breaks open and licks away.",
  "A mother cat licks each newborn clean, which also helps start its breathing, and she usually eats the placenta.",
  "Each kitten has its own placenta, and the queen normally passes one after each kitten is born.",
  "A queen usually delivers her kittens between ten minutes and an hour apart, though she can pause labour for hours.",
  "Newborn kittens weigh around 100 grams (about 3.5 ounces), roughly the weight of a bar of soap.",
  "Kittens are born with their eyes sealed shut and their ear canals closed, so at first they can neither see nor hear.",
  "A kitten's eyes usually open when it is between one and two weeks old.",
  "Kittens' eyes are blue when they first open, and their adult eye colour comes in gradually over the following months.",
  "Kittens' ear canals open during the second week of life, and their hearing sharpens over the following weeks.",
  "The umbilical cord stump on a newborn kitten dries up and falls off within about three days.",
  "Newborn kittens cannot keep themselves warm, so they huddle against their mother and each other for body heat.",
  "Newborn kittens sleep for about 90 percent of the day, waking mainly to nurse.",
  "Newborn kittens nurse every two to three hours around the clock in their first week or two.",
  "The mother's first milk, called colostrum, passes on antibodies that protect the kittens in their first weeks.",
  "Very young kittens cannot go to the toilet by themselves, so their mother licks them to make them go.",
  "Kittens can purr when they are only a few days old, often while they nurse.",
  "Kittens can crawl within their first few days but usually do not walk steadily until about three weeks old.",
  "A healthy kitten roughly doubles its birth weight by two weeks old.",
  "By about ten weeks old, a kitten often weighs around a kilogram (just over two pounds), ten times its birth weight.",
  "A kitten's milk teeth begin to come through at about two to three weeks of age.",
  "Kittens have 26 milk teeth. Adult cats have 30, fewer than a dog's 42 or a person's 32.",
  "Kittens lose their milk teeth and grow their adult set between about three and six months of age.",
  "Kittens start to try solid food at about four weeks old and are usually fully weaned by eight weeks.",
  "Kittens usually start using a litter tray at around three to four weeks old, often by copying their mother.",
  "Kittens begin grooming themselves at about four weeks old, and littermates soon start grooming each other.",
  "By five to six weeks, kittens are running, climbing and pouncing on their littermates.",
  "The key socialisation window for kittens runs from about two to seven weeks of age.",
  "Kittens handled gently by people during their first weeks tend to grow into more confident, friendly adults.",
  "Play-fighting with littermates teaches kittens how hard they can bite and swipe before the game stops.",
  "A mother cat carries her kittens by the scruff of the neck, and kittens go limp and quiet when held this way.",
  "Kittens should stay with their mother and littermates until at least eight weeks old. Many breeders wait until twelve.",
  "A kitten's protection from its mother's milk fades at around eight to twelve weeks, which is when vaccinations begin.",
  "Kittens usually have their first vaccinations at about eight or nine weeks old, with a second dose a month later.",
  "Many kittens have a pot belly from roundworms, and a wormy kitten can look well fed while really being thin.",
  "Roundworms are the most common worm in kittens, which can catch them through their mother's milk.",
  "Kittens are usually wormed every two weeks from about three weeks old, then monthly until they are six months old.",
  "Pale gums in a kitten can be a sign of anaemia caused by a heavy load of fleas or worms.",
  "Swallowed string or thread is a common kitten emergency, because it can cut into the gut as it is dragged along.",
  "A growing kitten needs roughly two to three times as many calories per kilogram as an adult cat.",
  "Kitten food is richer in calories, protein and certain minerals than adult food because kittens grow so fast.",
  "Kittens are usually fed three or four small meals a day until about six months old, then two meals a day.",
  "Kittens separated from their mother too early need a kitten milk replacer. Cow's milk does not have the right nutrients.",
  "A queen nursing a large litter may need two to three times her normal daily calories, so she is often fed kitten food.",
  "A pregnant cat's nipples turn pinker and more prominent at about three weeks, a change known as pinking up.",
  "A vet can often feel kittens in a pregnant queen's belly at three to four weeks, and an ultrasound can see them earlier.",
  "A queen can come back into heat and become pregnant again while she is still nursing her kittens.",
  "An unspayed cat can have two or even three litters a year.",
  "Female cats can come into heat and become pregnant as young as four to six months old, before they are fully grown.",
  "Most cats reach sexual maturity at around six months old, and females often get there before males.",
  "Male cats, called toms, can father kittens from around six months of age.",
  "Unspayed female cats come into heat again and again through spring and summer, roughly every two to three weeks.",
  "A heat lasts about a week on average, and if the cat does not mate, the cycle starts again a couple of weeks later.",
  "A female cat in heat does not usually bleed. Instead she becomes very vocal, restless and unusually affectionate.",
  "Cats are induced ovulators, meaning the act of mating itself triggers the release of eggs.",
  "Day length drives the cat's breeding season, so indoor cats living under artificial light may cycle all year round.",
  "Female cats do not go through menopause. An unspayed queen can keep having kittens into old age, though litters shrink.",
  "Spaying removes a female cat's ovaries and usually her uterus, which ends heat cycles and prevents pregnancy.",
  "Spaying a cat before her first heat greatly lowers her risk of mammary (breast) cancer later in life.",
  "Unspayed cats can develop pyometra, a serious infection of the womb, which spaying prevents completely.",
  "Neutering a male cat removes the testicles. It is a short operation and most cats go home the same day.",
  "Neutering a tom usually reduces roaming, fighting and the spraying of strong-smelling urine.",
  "Most vets neuter cats at around four to six months old, and many rescue centres do it from about eight weeks.",
  "Neutered cats need fewer calories than before, so their portions are usually reduced to prevent weight gain.",
  "Neutered cats live longer on average than unneutered ones, partly because they roam and fight less.",
  "Cats go through an adolescence from about six months to two years, physically mature but still bold and playful.",
  "Kittens are usually considered adults at about one year old, but some large cats keep filling out until three or four.",
  "A cat's growth plates, the soft zones at the ends of its bones, finish hardening at around one to one and a half years.",
  "The idea that one cat year equals seven human years is a myth. A one-year-old cat is more like a 15-year-old person.",
  "A two-year-old cat is roughly equivalent to a 24-year-old human, and each year after that adds about four human years.",
  "By that reckoning, a 10-year-old cat is about 56 in human years, and a 15-year-old is about 76.",
  "A cat reaching 20 is roughly like a person reaching 96.",
  "Cats are generally classed as mature from about 7, senior from about 11 and geriatric from about 15 years old.",
  "The average lifespan of a well-cared-for pet cat is about 12 to 15 years, and many live well into their late teens.",
  "Cats living only outdoors often survive just a few years, while indoor cats commonly live well into their teens.",
  "Indoor cats live longer mainly because they avoid cars, poisons, predators and diseases picked up from other cats.",
  "The oldest cats on record have lived into their late thirties, but anything past 20 is exceptional.",
  "Senior cats often sleep more, play less and lose some muscle along the back and hind legs.",
  "Older cats commonly groom themselves less thoroughly, so their coat may look duller or matted and need brushing.",
  "Many elderly cats develop arthritis, which usually shows as hesitation before jumping rather than an obvious limp.",
  "A cat that suddenly stops jumping onto its favourite high spots is often sore in its joints, not just getting lazy.",
  "Older cats' claws often grow thicker and more brittle, and need trimming more often to stop them curling into the pad.",
  "Vets suggest a check-up at least once a year for adult cats and twice a year for seniors.",
  "Many senior cats develop a bluish haze in the lens from normal ageing (lenticular sclerosis) that barely affects sight.",
  "Weight loss in an older cat is a common early sign of illness such as kidney disease, hyperthyroidism or diabetes.",
  "Some elderly cats show signs of dementia, such as yowling at night or seeming lost in familiar rooms.",
  "Hearing loss is common in very old cats, which can make them startle more easily and meow more loudly.",
  "Elderly cats with stiff joints find high-sided litter trays harder to climb into.",
  "Senior cat food is often lower in calories but not lower in protein, because older cats lose muscle easily.",
  "Cats with white or pale ears and noses can get sunburn, which over the years can turn into skin cancer on the ear tips.",
  "Cats are obligate carnivores: they are built to get key nutrients from meat and cannot thrive on a plant-only diet.",
  "Taurine is an amino acid cats cannot make enough of themselves, so it has to come from the meat in their diet.",
  "Without enough taurine, a cat can go blind from damage to the retina and develop a weak, enlarged heart.",
  "Dogs can make their own taurine, which is one reason dog food is not a safe long-term diet for a cat.",
  "Cats need a much higher share of protein in their food than dogs or people do.",
  "Cats cannot turn plant beta-carotene into vitamin A, so they need it ready-made from animal foods such as liver.",
  "Too much liver can give a cat vitamin A poisoning, which causes painful bony growths along the neck and spine.",
  "Cats cannot make arachidonic acid, a fatty acid found in animal fat, so it has to be in their food.",
  "Raw fish contains an enzyme that destroys vitamin B1, so a cat fed mostly raw fish can develop a serious deficiency.",
  "A diet of tinned tuna meant for people lacks nutrients a cat needs and can build up mercury over time.",
  "Most adult cats are lactose intolerant: after weaning they make much less lactase, the enzyme that digests milk sugar.",
  "Cow's milk gives many cats diarrhoea, so the traditional saucer of milk often does more harm than good.",
  "Cat milk sold in pet shops has had most of the lactose removed, which is why cats can digest it.",
  "Lilies are deadly to cats: even licking pollen off the fur or drinking the vase water can cause kidney failure.",
  "True lilies and day lilies attack the kidneys. Peace and calla lilies only irritate the mouth and are far less harmful.",
  "Onions, garlic, leeks and chives damage a cat's red blood cells and can cause anaemia, whether raw, cooked or powdered.",
  "Chocolate contains theobromine, which cats cannot break down well. Dark and baking chocolate are the most dangerous.",
  "Grapes and raisins have caused kidney failure in dogs and are treated as unsafe for cats as well.",
  "Xylitol, a sweetener in sugar-free gum, is toxic to dogs and is treated as unsafe for cats as a precaution.",
  "Paracetamol (acetaminophen), a common human painkiller, is extremely poisonous to cats. A single tablet can kill.",
  "Cats are so sensitive to paracetamol because they lack a liver enzyme that most mammals use to break it down.",
  "Human painkillers such as ibuprofen and aspirin are also toxic to cats, which cannot process them safely.",
  "Antifreeze containing ethylene glycol is deadly to cats. As little as a teaspoon can cause fatal kidney failure.",
  "Many essential oils, including tea tree, eucalyptus, peppermint and citrus oils, are toxic to cats, even when diffused.",
  "Raw bread dough is dangerous to cats: the yeast keeps rising in the warm stomach and produces alcohol as it ferments.",
  "Alcohol in any form is poisonous to cats, and their small size means a tiny amount can do serious harm.",
  "Caffeine, in coffee, tea and energy drinks, is toxic to cats in much the same way chocolate is.",
  "Many common houseplants are toxic to cats, including sago palm, dieffenbachia, pothos, cyclamen and azalea.",
  "Tulip and daffodil bulbs are poisonous to cats. Poinsettia, by contrast, is only mildly irritating despite its bad name.",
  "Lily of the valley is also poisonous to cats, but it affects the heart rather than the kidneys.",
  "Permethrin, found in many dog flea treatments, is highly toxic to cats, so dog flea products are never safe on a cat.",
  "Disinfectants containing phenol are especially dangerous to cats, which lick any residue off their paws and fur.",
  "Rodent poisons can kill a cat either directly or when the cat eats a poisoned mouse.",
  "Cooked bones can splinter and lodge in a cat's throat or gut, which is why they are left out of cat diets.",
  "Raw eggs and raw meat can carry salmonella and other bacteria that make cats, and the people around them, ill.",
  "Cats descend from desert animals and have a weak thirst drive, so they tend to drink little water.",
  "A cat's kidneys are very good at concentrating urine, which is how cats can survive on so little water.",
  "Wet cat food is about 75 to 80 percent water, so cats eating it get much of their daily water from their meals.",
  "Cats fed only dry food drink more from the bowl than cats on wet food, but still take in less water overall.",
  "Many cats prefer running water, which is why a dripping tap or a pet fountain can tempt a reluctant drinker.",
  "Many cats dislike drinking right beside their food, and drink more when the water bowl sits in a separate spot.",
  "Signs of dehydration in a cat include sticky gums and skin that is slow to spring back when gently lifted.",
  "A typical 4 kilogram (9 pound) indoor cat needs only around 200 to 250 calories a day, about one small bagel's worth.",
  "Cats are built to eat many small meals, often ten or more a day, rather than one or two big ones.",
  "Treats should make up no more than about 10 percent of a cat's daily calories.",
  "Obesity is the most common nutritional problem in pet cats. Surveys suggest around half of pet cats are overweight.",
  "A cat is classed as obese when it is about 20 percent or more above its ideal weight.",
  "An extra kilogram on a 4 kilogram cat is, in proportion, like an extra 20 kilograms on an average adult.",
  "A healthy cat has a visible waist from above and ribs that can be felt under a thin layer of fat but not seen.",
  "Overweight cats are far more likely to develop diabetes, arthritis and urinary problems, and tend to die younger.",
  "A cat that stops eating or slims too fast can develop hepatic lipidosis, a dangerous build-up of fat in the liver.",
  "Safe weight loss for a cat is slow, no more than about 1 to 2 percent of body weight a week.",
  "Leaving dry food out all day is a common cause of weight gain in indoor cats, which have little chance to burn it off.",
  "Puzzle feeders make a cat work for its food, which helps with both weight control and boredom.",
  "Cat grass (usually wheat or oat grass) is safe for cats to nibble and is sold for indoor cats that like greenery.",
  "Dental disease is one of the most common health problems in cats. Most cats over three show some sign of gum disease.",
  "Cats rarely get cavities the way people do. Their main dental problems are tartar, gum disease and tooth resorption.",
  "Tooth resorption, where the body slowly dissolves a tooth from the inside, is common in adult cats and is painful.",
  "Bad breath in a cat is usually a sign of dental disease rather than of something it ate.",
  "Cats with sore mouths often keep eating but swallow dry food whole or drop it, so dental pain is easy to miss.",
  "Many cats accept tooth brushing with a cat toothpaste if it is introduced slowly. Human toothpaste is not safe for them.",
  "Cats get dental cleaning under general anaesthetic, because tartar below the gumline cannot be removed otherwise.",
  "Hairballs start with grooming: the tiny hooks on a cat's tongue pull out loose fur, which the cat then swallows.",
  "Most swallowed fur passes through a cat harmlessly. A hairball is the fur that stays in the stomach and comes back up.",
  "A hairball is usually sausage-shaped, not round, because it is squeezed through the narrow food pipe on its way out.",
  "Long-haired cats, and any cat shedding heavily in spring, produce more hairballs. Regular brushing cuts them down.",
  "Hairballs more than about once a month can signal over-grooming or a digestive problem rather than normal shedding.",
  "Hairball remedies lubricate the gut or add fibre, so that swallowed fur passes through instead of coming back up.",
  "A cat's coughing fits are often mistaken for attempts to bring up a hairball, but they can be a sign of feline asthma.",
  "A single flea can lay around 50 eggs a day, and the eggs drop off the cat into carpets and bedding.",
  "Only about 5 percent of a flea problem is adult fleas on the cat. The rest is eggs, larvae and pupae around the home.",
  "Indoor cats can still get fleas, which arrive on clothing, shoes or visiting pets.",
  "Fleas can pass tapeworms to cats: a cat swallows an infected flea while grooming and the worm grows in its gut.",
  "Cats allergic to flea bites can get sore, scabby skin from just one or two bites, especially along the back and neck.",
  "A heavy flea infestation can cause anaemia in a kitten, because the fleas drink so much of its blood.",
  "Cat scratch disease is caused by a bacterium that cats pick up from fleas, and kittens are the most likely to carry it.",
  "Cats that hunt need worming more often than indoor cats, because mice and birds carry worm larvae.",
  "Ear mites are a common cause of itchy ears in kittens and leave a dark, crumbly discharge much like coffee grounds.",
  "Ringworm in cats is a fungus, not a worm. It shows as circular bald patches and can spread to people.",
  "Core cat vaccines cover panleukopenia (a deadly virus) and the two main causes of cat flu, herpesvirus and calicivirus.",
  "Feline panleukopenia is caused by a parvovirus closely related to the one that causes parvo in dogs.",
  "The panleukopenia virus can survive in the environment for up to a year, so even indoor cats are vaccinated against it.",
  "Cat flu viruses can leave a cat as a lifelong carrier that flares up under stress, much like cold sores in people.",
  "After the kitten course, most cats have a booster at one year old, then every one to three years depending on the jab.",
  "Rabies vaccination is required by law for cats in many places, including much of the United States.",
  "FIV, feline immunodeficiency virus, is a cat-only relative of HIV and cannot infect people.",
  "FIV spreads mainly through deep bite wounds, so unneutered outdoor toms that fight are the most often infected.",
  "Many FIV-positive cats live long, normal lives, especially when kept indoors and away from other infections.",
  "Feline leukaemia virus (FeLV) spreads through close contact like grooming and shared bowls, and from mother to kitten.",
  "A vaccine exists for feline leukaemia virus, and it is often recommended for kittens and for cats that go outdoors.",
  "Both FIV and FeLV can be detected with a quick blood test at the vet.",
  "Chronic kidney disease is very common in old cats, and the risk climbs steeply after about the age of ten.",
  "The earliest signs of kidney disease in cats are usually drinking more and peeing more, followed by weight loss.",
  "Kidney disease in cats often causes no outward signs until about two thirds of kidney function has already been lost.",
  "Kidney disease in cats cannot be cured, but special diets and fluids often give affected cats good extra years.",
  "Cats with kidney disease are often fed a diet lower in phosphorus, which slows the progress of the disease.",
  "Cats get a form of diabetes much like type 2 in people, and overweight middle-aged male cats are at the highest risk.",
  "Most diabetic cats are treated with insulin injections at home twice a day, which most cats tolerate well.",
  "With insulin and a low-carbohydrate diet, some diabetic cats go into remission and no longer need injections.",
  "A diabetic cat that walks on its hocks (ankles) instead of its toes may have nerve damage from high blood sugar.",
  "Hyperthyroidism, an overactive thyroid gland, is the most common hormone disorder in older cats.",
  "A hyperthyroid cat typically eats ravenously yet loses weight, and may be restless, vocal and have a racing heart.",
  "Hyperthyroidism in cats can often be cured outright with a single treatment of radioactive iodine.",
  "Hyperthyroidism was almost unknown in cats before the late 1970s, and why it became so common is not fully understood.",
  "High blood pressure is common in older cats, often alongside kidney disease or hyperthyroidism, and can cause blindness.",
  "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a thickening of the heart muscle, is the most common heart disease in cats.",
  "A urinary blockage is an emergency in male cats, whose narrow urethra can be plugged by crystals or mucus within hours.",
  "Cat bites to people get infected far more often than dog bites, as the needle-like teeth push bacteria deep into tissue.",
  "A microchip is about the size of a grain of rice and is injected under the skin between the shoulder blades.",
  "A microchip has no battery. A scanner powers it for a moment and reads a unique number linked to the owner's details.",
  "Microchipping has been compulsory for pet cats in England since June 2024.",
  "Some cat flaps and feeders read a cat's microchip and open only for that cat.",
  "Indoor cats need outlets for hunting behaviour: toys that move, scratching posts, high perches and places to hide.",
  "Cats use vertical space, so shelves and tall perches effectively enlarge a cat's territory in a small home.",
  "Several short play sessions a day suit cats better than one long one, matching the rhythm of hunting in short bursts.",
  "A study of shelter cats found that having a cardboard box to hide in helped them settle into a new place faster.",
  "The usual rule for litter trays is one per cat plus one extra, placed in different quiet spots around the home.",
  "Most cats prefer unscented, fine-grained clumping litter and a tray big enough to turn around in comfortably.",
  "A dirty or badly placed litter tray is one of the most common reasons a cat starts toileting elsewhere in the home.",
  "Short-haired cats need brushing about once a week. Long-haired cats need combing daily to stop painful mats forming.",
  "Mats in a long coat pull on the skin and can trap moisture and dirt, leading to sores and infection underneath.",
  "Cats spend a large share of their waking hours grooming, often estimated at 30 to 50 percent.",
  "Cats shed most heavily in spring and autumn as the coat changes. Indoor cats shed more evenly all year round.",
  "Most cats never need a bath: their own grooming keeps them clean, and bathing strips protective oils from the coat.",
  "Hairless cats are the exception: with no fur to absorb skin oils, they need regular baths or wipes.",
  "A cat that gets something sticky, oily or toxic on its coat does need washing, with a cat shampoo and lukewarm water.",
  "A cat's claws grow continuously, and the old outer layer is shed as a hollow sheath, often found near scratching posts.",
  "Only the clear tip of a claw is cut when trimming. The pink area inside (the quick) holds blood vessels and nerves.",
  "Cats have a fifth claw on each front paw, the dewclaw, which does not touch the ground and can overgrow if not trimmed.",
  "Cats hide pain well: a cat in pain often simply withdraws, hides, grooms less and sits hunched with half-closed eyes.",
  "The Feline Grimace Scale lets vets score a cat's pain from its ears, eyes, muzzle, whiskers and head position.",
  "Going off food, hiding and a scruffy, unkempt coat are among the earliest signs that a cat is unwell.",
  "A cat's normal body temperature is about 38 to 39 C (100.5 to 102.5 F), a degree or two warmer than a person's.",
  "A resting cat's heart beats around 140 to 220 times a minute, two to three times faster than an adult human's.",
  "A cat takes about 20 to 30 breaths a minute at rest, compared with 12 to 20 for an adult person.",
  "The first cloned cat, named CC (short for Copy Cat), was born in Texas in December 2001.",
  "CC the cloned cat did not look exactly like her genetic donor, because a cat's coat pattern is partly set in the womb.",
  "CC the cloned cat lived a normal life, had kittens of her own and died in 2020 at the age of 18.",
  "The first cat genome to be sequenced belonged to an Abyssinian named Cinnamon, and it was published in 2007.",
  "Cat genome research helps human medicine too, because cats get many of the same inherited diseases as people.",
  "There are thought to be several hundred million pet cats in the world.",
  "In several countries, including Germany and Russia, pet cats outnumber pet dogs.",
  "Most cat allergies are to a protein called Fel d 1, which is made in the cat's saliva and skin glands, not in the fur.",
  "Cats spread Fel d 1 onto their fur when they groom, and the dried flakes float in the air and settle on furniture.",
  "Male cats produce more Fel d 1 than females, and neutered males produce less than unneutered ones.",
  "No cat is truly hypoallergenic, though some individual cats produce much less Fel d 1 than others.",
  "A cat food now exists that binds Fel d 1 in the cat's saliva using egg antibodies, reducing the allergen on its fur.",
  "Cats are not truly nocturnal: they are crepuscular, most active around dawn and dusk when their prey is about.",
  "A cat's tail is part of its spine, so a hard pull or a slammed door can damage the nerves that control the bladder.",
  "Tail-pull injuries are common in cats hit by cars, when the tail is caught under a wheel as the cat runs.",
  "Cats can live well without a tail after an amputation for injury, though they take a while to adjust their balance.",
  "Declawing is not a nail trim: it amputates the last bone of each toe, the one the claw grows from.",
  "Declawing is illegal in the UK, much of Europe, Australia and New Zealand, and in several US states and cities.",
  "New York became the first US state to ban declawing, in 2019.",
  "Studies have linked declawing to higher rates of biting and litter tray avoidance in the cats that had it done.",
  "Cats pass the toxoplasmosis parasite in their faeces, but usually only for a couple of weeks after first catching it.",
  "Toxoplasma in cat faeces takes a day or more to become infectious, so a litter tray scooped daily carries little risk.",
  "People are more likely to catch toxoplasmosis from undercooked meat or unwashed vegetables than from their own cat.",
  "Pregnant women are advised to let someone else clean the litter tray, or to wear gloves and wash their hands afterwards.",
  "Most cats with toxoplasmosis show no signs at all, and once infected they rarely shed the parasite again.",
}

-- Breeds and the cat fancy (320)
add {
  "The Abyssinian is named after Abyssinia, the old name for Ethiopia, but the breed was developed in Britain.",
  "Abyssinians have a ticked coat, with several bands of colour on each hair, giving a warm shimmering look.",
  "A cat named Zula, brought to England from Abyssinia in the 1860s, is often cited as the Abyssinian's founder.",
  "The American Bobtail is a naturally short-tailed breed that was developed in the United States from the 1960s.",
  "An American Bobtail's tail is short but clearly visible, and the standard says it should not reach below the hock.",
  "The American Bobtail was bred to look like a bobcat, but it is a fully domestic cat with no wild blood.",
  "The American Curl's ears curl backwards in a smooth arc, a natural mutation first seen in California in 1981.",
  "The first American Curl was a stray longhaired black kitten named Shulamith, found in Lakewood, California.",
  "American Curl kittens are born with straight ears that begin to curl within days and set by about four months.",
  "An American Curl's ears should curl in an arc of at least 90 degrees but no more than 180 degrees.",
  "The American Shorthair descends from cats brought to North America by early European settlers to control rats.",
  "Until 1966 the American Shorthair was called the Domestic Shorthair, a name changed to set it apart from moggies.",
  "The American Shorthair was among the first five breeds recognised by the CFA when the association began in 1906.",
  "The silver classic tabby is the best known American Shorthair, though the breed has over 80 colours and patterns.",
  "The American Wirehair began with one kitten born on a farm in upstate New York in 1966 with a springy, wiry coat.",
  "An American Wirehair's hairs are crimped, hooked or bent, down to the whiskers, so the coat feels like steel wool.",
  "The American Wirehair is one of the rarest breeds, and its coat mutation has only been recorded in the United States.",
  "The Australian Mist was developed in Australia from the 1970s by crossing Burmese, Abyssinian and domestic cats.",
  "The Australian Mist was first called the Spotted Mist, and was renamed when marbled cats joined the spotted ones.",
  "The Australian Mist was the first pedigree cat breed developed in Australia.",
  "The Balinese is a longhaired Siamese, named for the graceful dancers of Bali, not for any link to the island.",
  "The Balinese grew from longhaired kittens born in Siamese litters, and was established in the US in the 1950s.",
  "Balinese cats have the pointed colouring and blue eyes of the Siamese, with a silky single coat and a plumed tail.",
  "The Bengal was bred in the United States by crossing domestic cats with the Asian leopard cat, a small wild cat.",
  "The Bengal's name comes from Prionailurus bengalensis, the scientific name of the Asian leopard cat.",
  "Bengals must be several generations removed from their wild ancestor before registries allow them to be shown.",
  "Bengals come in spotted and marbled patterns, and the rosette, a spot with a darker outline, is a prized marking.",
  "The Birman is also called the Sacred Cat of Burma, after a legend about temple cats whose paws stayed pure white.",
  "Birmans have a pointed coat, deep blue eyes and four pure white paws that breeders call gloves.",
  "The white on a Birman's back paws runs up the back of the leg in a point, a marking that breeders call laces.",
  "The Birman was first recognised as a breed in France in 1925.",
  "The Bombay was created in Kentucky in the 1950s by crossing Burmese with black American Shorthairs.",
  "The Bombay is named after the Indian city of Bombay, now Mumbai, as a nod to the black leopards of India.",
  "A Bombay has a jet black coat right to the roots, copper or gold eyes and a sheen often compared to patent leather.",
  "The British Longhair is the longhaired version of the British Shorthair, and the two can be born in the same litter.",
  "The British Longhair's coat comes from Persians crossed into the British Shorthair to rebuild it after the world wars.",
  "The British Shorthair is one of the oldest British breeds, developed from street and farm cats and shown in the 1870s.",
  "The British Blue, with its dense grey-blue coat and copper eyes, is the most famous colour of the British Shorthair.",
  "The British Shorthair has been the most popular pedigree breed registered with the GCCF in Britain for many years.",
  "British Shorthairs mature slowly and may not reach their full size until they are three to five years old.",
  "The Burmese breed in the West descends from a brown cat named Wong Mau, brought from Burma to San Francisco in 1930.",
  "Wong Mau, the founding Burmese, turned out to be a Burmese-Siamese hybrid of the kind now called Tonkinese.",
  "The classic Burmese colour is sable, a rich dark brown, which is called brown in Britain.",
  "Burmese cats have a heavy, muscular body under a short satin coat, often described as a brick wrapped in silk.",
  "The Burmilla came from an accidental mating between a Chinchilla Persian and a lilac Burmese in Britain in 1981.",
  "The Burmilla's name combines Burmese and Chinchilla, the two breeds behind it.",
  "Burmillas have a shaded or tipped silver coat and dark lines around the eyes that look like eyeliner.",
  "The Chartreux is an old French breed with a blue-grey woolly coat and copper to orange eyes.",
  "The Chartreux is known for its smiling expression, which comes from its rounded head and tapered muzzle.",
  "Legend links the Chartreux to the Carthusian monks of France, but there is no proof the monks ever bred the cats.",
  "The Chartreux is traditionally a quiet breed, and many of these cats chirp rather than meow.",
  "The Chausie's name comes from Felis chaus, the scientific name of the jungle cat, one of its ancestors.",
  "The Chausie is a tall, long-legged breed developed from crosses between domestic cats and the wild jungle cat.",
  "Chausies are accepted in only three colours: solid black, black grizzled ticked tabby and brown ticked tabby.",
  "The Cornish Rex began in 1950 with a curly-coated kitten named Kallibunker, born on a farm in Cornwall, England.",
  "The Cornish Rex has only soft undercoat hairs, with no guard hairs, and they lie in tight waves like a washboard.",
  "The word Rex in curly-coated cat breed names was borrowed from rex rabbits, which have a similar plush coat.",
  "Cornish Rex cats have an arched back, tucked-up waist and long legs, giving them the outline of a small greyhound.",
  "The Cymric is the longhaired Manx, and its name is the Welsh word for Welsh, though the breed is from the Isle of Man.",
  "Some registries, including the CFA, treat the Cymric as a longhaired division of the Manx, not a separate breed.",
  "The Devon Rex began in 1960 with a curly kitten named Kirlee, born near a disused tin mine in Devon, England.",
  "Mating the first Devon Rex to Cornish Rex cats gave only straight-coated kittens, proving the two curl genes differ.",
  "The Devon Rex is often called a pixie cat because of its large low-set ears, wide eyes and short muzzle.",
  "Devon Rex whiskers are often short, curly and brittle, and some cats appear to have almost no whiskers at all.",
  "The Donskoy is a hairless breed from Russia, first found in 1987 in Rostov-on-Don, the city it is named after.",
  "Unlike the Sphynx, the Donskoy owes its hairlessness to a dominant gene, so one hairless parent can pass it on.",
  "The Donskoy breed started with a kitten named Varvara, rescued from boys in Rostov-on-Don, who lost her fur as she grew.",
  "The Donskoy is also known as the Don Sphynx, though it is not related to the Canadian Sphynx.",
  "The Egyptian Mau is one of the few naturally spotted domestic breeds, with spots on the coat and on the skin beneath it.",
  "Mau is the ancient Egyptian word for cat, which gives the Egyptian Mau its name.",
  "The Egyptian Mau carries a mark on its forehead that fanciers call the scarab beetle mark, shaped like the letter M.",
  "An exiled Russian princess, Nathalie Troubetskoy, brought the first Egyptian Maus from Italy to the US in 1956.",
  "The European Shorthair is the pedigree form of the ordinary house cat of mainland Europe, with its own FIFe standard.",
  "Until 1982 FIFe grouped the European Shorthair with the British Shorthair under a single standard.",
  "The European Shorthair is less extreme than the British Shorthair, with a longer face and a less heavy body.",
  "The Exotic Shorthair is a shorthaired Persian, nicknamed the lazy man's Persian because its coat needs less grooming.",
  "The Exotic Shorthair was created in the US in the 1950s and 1960s by crossing Persians with American Shorthairs.",
  "Exotic Shorthairs are judged to the same standard as the Persian in every respect except the coat.",
  "The Exotic Shorthair was accepted for championship by the CFA in 1967.",
  "The Havana Brown is a solid chocolate brown cat developed in England in the 1950s from Siamese and black domestic cats.",
  "The Havana Brown may be named for the colour of Havana cigars or after the Havana rabbit, which has the same brown coat.",
  "The Havana Brown is one of the few breeds whose standard requires the whiskers to be brown to match the coat.",
  "The Havana Brown has a distinctive muzzle that the standard compares to a corn cob or a light bulb.",
  "The Highlander is a recent breed with curled ears and a naturally short tail, developed in the US in the 2000s.",
  "The Highlander was known as the Highland Lynx until it was renamed in 2005.",
  "The Highlander has no wild cat blood despite its lynx-like look, which was bred in from domestic cats alone.",
  "The Himalayan is a Persian with the Siamese pointed pattern and blue eyes, created by crossing the two breeds.",
  "The Himalayan is named after the Himalayan rabbit, which has the same dark points on a pale body, not the mountains.",
  "In the CFA the Himalayan is a colour division of the Persian, while some other registries list it as a breed of its own.",
  "In Britain the Himalayan is called the Colourpoint Persian.",
  "The Japanese Bobtail's short pom-pom tail comes from a recessive gene, and every tail is unique in its kinks and curves.",
  "The Japanese Bobtail has lived in Japan for centuries and appears in old Japanese paintings and woodblock prints.",
  "The mi-ke, or tricolour, of white with patches of black and red is the most famous Japanese Bobtail colour.",
  "The Japanese Bobtail was brought to the United States in 1968 by Elizabeth Freret, and the CFA recognised it in 1976.",
  "The Javanese is a longhaired Siamese-type cat in colours the Balinese does not allow, named after the island of Java.",
  "In 2008 the CFA merged the Javanese into the Balinese breed as a colour division.",
  "The Khao Manee is an ancient pure white breed from Thailand, and its name means white gem.",
  "Khao Manee cats often have odd eyes, one blue and one gold or green, which breeders prize highly.",
  "The Khao Manee is described in the Tamra Maew, an old Thai book of cat poems.",
  "In Thailand the Khao Manee is also known as the Diamond Eye cat.",
  "The Korat is a silver-blue cat from Thailand, named after Korat province, and is one of the oldest stable breeds.",
  "In Thailand the Korat is called Si-Sawat and is traditionally given in pairs to brides as a token of good luck.",
  "A Korat's blue coat is tipped with silver, which gives it a halo effect, and its eyes are a luminous green.",
  "The Korat has a heart-shaped head when viewed from the front, a feature called for in the breed standard.",
  "The Kurilian Bobtail is a natural breed from the Kuril Islands, a chain of islands between Russia and Japan.",
  "The Kurilian Bobtail's pom-pom tail has between two and ten vertebrae, kinked and curved so that no two are the same.",
  "The Kurilian Bobtail is popular in Russia and recognised by FIFe and TICA, but it is still rare in North America.",
  "The LaPerm began in 1982 with a bald kitten named Curly, born on a farm in Oregon, who later grew a curly coat.",
  "The LaPerm's curly coat is caused by a dominant gene, unlike the recessive curls of the Cornish and Devon Rex.",
  "LaPerm kittens may be born bald or straight-coated and then grow curls, and some lose and regrow their coat.",
  "The Lykoi, or werewolf cat, has a patchy coat with no hair on the face mask, from a natural mutation in domestic cats.",
  "The Lykoi's name comes from the Greek word for wolves.",
  "The Lykoi breed was founded in 2011 in Tennessee from kittens born to feral and shelter cats carrying the gene.",
  "The Lykoi's coat is roan, a mix of coloured and white hairs that is common in horses but found in no other cat breed.",
  "The Maine Coon is the official state cat of Maine, where it developed as a hardy farm cat.",
  "The Maine Coon is one of the largest domestic breeds, with a shaggy coat, a bushy tail and tufted ears and toes.",
  "Legend says the Maine Coon descends from a cat and a raccoon, which is impossible, but the name stuck.",
  "A Maine Coon named Cosey won the top prize at the first big American cat show, held at Madison Square Garden in 1895.",
  "The Manx comes from the Isle of Man, where its tailless gene spread through an isolated island cat population.",
  "Manx cats are classed by tail: rumpy (no tail), rumpy riser (a small bump), stumpy (short) and longy (full tail).",
  "The Manx tailless gene is dominant, and kittens with two copies do not survive, so Manx litters are often small.",
  "Some Manx cats have a spinal condition called Manx syndrome, which is linked to the gene that shortens the tail.",
  "The Munchkin is a short-legged breed caused by a natural dominant mutation that leaves the body a normal size.",
  "The Munchkin breed was founded in Louisiana in 1983 from a short-legged stray named Blackberry.",
  "The Munchkin is named after the small people of Oz in L. Frank Baum's book The Wonderful Wizard of Oz.",
  "TICA accepted the Munchkin into its new breed programme in 1994, but the CFA, GCCF and FIFe do not recognise it.",
  "The Nebelung is a longhaired blue cat with green eyes, bred to look like a longhaired Russian Blue.",
  "The Nebelung's name comes from the German word Nebel, meaning mist or fog, for its soft blue-grey coat.",
  "The Nebelung breed began in Denver in the 1980s with two longhaired blue kittens named Siegfried and Brunhilde.",
  "The Norwegian Forest Cat has a water-shedding top coat over a woolly undercoat, built for Scandinavian winters.",
  "In Norway the Norwegian Forest Cat is called the skogkatt, which simply means forest cat.",
  "The Norwegian Forest Cat was accepted by FIFe in 1977 and reached the United States in 1979.",
  "The Norwegian Forest Cat has a triangular head with a straight profile, which sets it apart from the Maine Coon.",
  "The Ocicat is a spotted breed with no wild blood that appeared by accident in 1964 from Siamese and Abyssinian lines.",
  "The Ocicat is named after the ocelot, a spotted wild cat, because of its look, although the two are unrelated.",
  "The first Ocicat was named Tonga, and the breed was built from Siamese, Abyssinian and American Shorthair cats.",
  "The breeder of the first Ocicat was trying to make an Abyssinian-pointed Siamese when a spotted kitten appeared.",
  "The Oriental Shorthair is a Siamese-type cat in colours and patterns other than pointed, usually with green eyes.",
  "Oriental Shorthairs come in more than 300 colour and pattern combinations.",
  "The Oriental Shorthair was developed in Britain in the 1950s and 1960s from Siamese crossed with other breeds.",
  "In Britain, solid-coloured Orientals were first given their own names, such as Havana for chocolate and Foreign White.",
  "The Persian is one of the oldest and most popular pedigree breeds, and was shown at the first cat show in 1871.",
  "The Persian's name refers to Persia, now Iran, from where longhaired cats were first brought to Europe in the 1600s.",
  "The Persian is known for its long flowing coat, round face and short nose, and comes in a huge range of colours.",
  "The Persian's flat face can make breathing and tear drainage harder, so many breeders now favour a more moderate head.",
  "The Persian was the most registered breed with the CFA for many years.",
  "The Peterbald was created in St Petersburg, Russia, in 1994 by crossing a Donskoy with an Oriental Shorthair.",
  "The Peterbald has a long, slender Oriental build and may be bald, flocked, velour or brush-coated.",
  "Peterbald kittens can be born with hair and lose it as they grow, because the hair-loss gene is dominant and variable.",
  "The Pixie-bob is a bobtailed breed from the Pacific Northwest, bred to look like a bobcat, though it is fully domestic.",
  "The Pixie-bob breed is named after a kitten called Pixie, born in 1986 to a bobtailed cat in Washington state.",
  "The Pixie-bob is one of the few breeds whose standard allows polydactyl cats, with up to seven toes on a foot.",
  "The Pixie-bob is shown only as a brown spotted tabby, often with a woolly coat and a heavy brow that gives a wild look.",
  "The Ragamuffin split from the Ragdoll breed in 1994 and allows a wider range of colours and patterns than the Ragdoll.",
  "The Ragamuffin gained championship status in the CFA in 2011.",
  "Unlike the Ragdoll, the Ragamuffin may be any colour and pattern, with or without white, and need not have blue eyes.",
  "The Ragdoll was developed in California in the 1960s by Ann Baker, and is named for the way many go limp when picked up.",
  "Ragdolls are pointed cats with blue eyes, and they come in three patterns: colourpoint, mitted and bicolour.",
  "All Ragdoll kittens are born white and develop their colour over the first weeks, with the full colour taking years.",
  "The Ragdoll's founder was a white longhaired cat named Josephine, who lived in Riverside, California.",
  "In recent years the Ragdoll has been the most registered breed with the CFA, ahead of the Maine Coon and Exotic.",
  "The Russian Blue has a dense, silver-tipped blue coat and vivid green eyes.",
  "The Russian Blue is thought to come from the port of Arkhangelsk in Russia, and was once called the Archangel Cat.",
  "The Russian Blue was shown at the Crystal Palace in London in 1875 under the name Archangel Cat.",
  "Russian Blues were crossed with Siamese during the Second World War when numbers fell, then bred back to the old type.",
  "Russian Blues have mauve or lavender-pink paw pads, unlike the grey pads of most other blue cats.",
  "The Savannah is a cross between domestic cats and the serval, a tall, long-legged wild cat of Africa.",
  "The first Savannah, herself named Savannah, was born in 1986 to a Siamese mother and a serval father.",
  "TICA accepted the Savannah for championship status in 2012.",
  "Savannahs are labelled by generation: an F1 cat has a serval parent, an F2 a serval grandparent, and so on.",
  "Owning a Savannah is restricted or banned in some places because of its wild ancestry, often depending on generation.",
  "The Scottish Fold descends from a barn cat named Susie, found in Scotland in 1961 with ears that folded forward.",
  "The Scottish Fold's ears come from a dominant gene that affects cartilage throughout the body, not only in the ears.",
  "Scottish Fold kittens are born with straight ears, and the fold appears at about three weeks in those with the gene.",
  "The GCCF stopped registering Scottish Folds in the early 1970s over concerns about cartilage and joint problems.",
  "Scottish Folds with straight ears are called Scottish Straights, and breeders use them to avoid pairing two folds.",
  "The Selkirk Rex has a loose, curly coat and a sturdy body, and is sometimes called the cat in sheep's clothing.",
  "The Selkirk Rex began in 1987 with a curly kitten from a Montana animal shelter, later named Miss DePesto.",
  "The Selkirk Rex is said to be the only cat breed named after a person: its founder named it after her stepfather.",
  "Unlike the Cornish and Devon Rex, the Selkirk Rex curl gene is dominant, so one curly parent can give curly kittens.",
  "The Siamese comes from Thailand, formerly Siam, where pointed cats were described in old manuscripts.",
  "Siamese cats shown at the Crystal Palace in 1871 were described by one writer as an unnatural nightmare kind of cat.",
  "The four classic Siamese colours are seal, chocolate, blue and lilac point.",
  "Many early Siamese had crossed eyes and kinked tails, which are now considered faults in the show ring.",
  "The Siamese is an ancestor of many breeds, including the Balinese, Oriental, Himalayan, Tonkinese and Snowshoe.",
  "The Siberian is Russia's native forest cat, with a thick triple coat, and is often called the national cat of Russia.",
  "The Siberian was first exported from Russia in 1990, after the opening up of the Soviet Union made it possible.",
  "The Neva Masquerade is the colourpoint version of the Siberian, named for the Neva river in St Petersburg.",
  "The Siberian is slow to mature and can take about five years to reach its full size.",
  "The Singapura is one of the smallest breeds, with a sepia ticked coat and large eyes and ears for its size.",
  "The Singapura takes its name from the Malay name for Singapore, where its ancestors lived as street cats.",
  "The Singapura is shown in only one colour, sepia agouti, a warm ivory ticked with dark brown.",
  "The Singapura was brought to the United States in the 1970s by Hal and Tommy Meadow, and the CFA recognised it in 1988.",
  "The Snowshoe is a pointed cat with white feet, bred in Philadelphia in the 1960s from Siamese and American Shorthairs.",
  "The Snowshoe's white markings are hard to breed to the standard, which is one reason the breed remains rare.",
  "Snowshoes usually have an inverted V of white on the face and white boots on all four feet.",
  "The Sokoke is a rare natural breed from the coast of Kenya, named after the Arabuko Sokoke forest.",
  "The Sokoke has a blotched tabby coat with ticked hairs, giving a look often compared to wood grain or tree bark.",
  "The Sokoke was first bred from cats found in Kenya in 1978 by Jeni Slater, and was taken to Denmark in the 1980s.",
  "The Somali is the longhaired version of the Abyssinian, with a full brush tail that earns it the nickname fox cat.",
  "The Somali was named after Somalia, Ethiopia's neighbour, to echo the Abyssinian's name.",
  "The Somali was developed in the 1960s and 1970s from longhaired kittens that sometimes appeared in Abyssinian litters.",
  "The Sphynx is a hairless breed that began in Toronto in 1966 with a hairless kitten named Prune.",
  "The Sphynx is not truly bald: its skin is covered in fine down that feels like warm suede or a peach.",
  "The Sphynx's hairlessness comes from a recessive gene, and another form of the same gene gives the Devon Rex its coat.",
  "A Sphynx's skin shows the colour and pattern that the cat's fur would have had.",
  "The Sphynx was granted championship status by the CFA in 2002.",
  "The Thai is TICA's name for the old-style Siamese, with a rounder head and sturdier body than the modern show Siamese.",
  "In Thailand the Thai cat is called the Wichienmaat, a name also used there for the Siamese.",
  "The Thai is sometimes called the Old-Style, Classic or Applehead Siamese.",
  "The Tonkinese is a cross between the Siamese and the Burmese, blending the colouring and build of both.",
  "Tonkinese come in three patterns: pointed, mink and solid, and mink cats have aqua eyes.",
  "The Tonkinese is named after the Tonkin region of Vietnam, although the breed has no connection to it.",
  "When two mink Tonkinese are bred, on average half the kittens are mink, a quarter pointed and a quarter solid.",
  "The Tonkinese was recognised in 1971 by the Canadian Cat Association, the first registry to accept it.",
  "The Toyger is a striped breed designed to look like a tiny tiger, created in the United States from the 1980s.",
  "The Toyger was developed from Bengals and striped domestic shorthairs, and has no tiger blood at all.",
  "The Toyger's name is a blend of the words toy and tiger.",
  "TICA accepted the Toyger for championship status in 2007.",
  "The Turkish Angora is an ancient longhaired breed from Turkey, named after Ankara, which was once called Angora.",
  "Ankara Zoo has run a breeding programme for many years to preserve the white Turkish Angora.",
  "Turkish Angoras almost vanished as a pure breed in the early 1900s after being crossed with Persians.",
  "White Turkish Angoras with blue or odd eyes are the best known, and the CFA accepted only white cats until 1978.",
  "The Turkish Van comes from the Lake Van region of eastern Turkey and is famous for liking to swim.",
  "The Turkish Van's white body with colour only on the head and tail is so distinctive it is called the van pattern.",
  "Turkish Vans often have odd eyes, one blue and one amber.",
  "The Turkish Van was brought to Britain in 1955 by two photographers who were given a pair of kittens in Turkey.",
  "The Ukrainian Levkoy is a hairless breed with folded ears, created in Ukraine from Donskoy and Scottish Fold crosses.",
  "The Ukrainian Levkoy is named after the levkoy plant, whose bent leaves are said to resemble the cat's folded ears.",
  "The Ukrainian Levkoy is recognised by Ukrainian and Russian clubs but not yet by the major international registries.",
  "The York Chocolate is a longhaired chocolate or lilac breed developed in New York State in the 1980s from farm cats.",
  "The York Chocolate began with a kitten named Brownie, born in 1983 to a black longhaired farm cat.",
  "The York Chocolate has been recognised by only a few registries, and the breed is now very rare.",
  "The first proper cat show was held at the Crystal Palace in London in July 1871, organised by the artist Harrison Weir.",
  "Harrison Weir, who organised the first cat show, wrote the first breed standards, which he called Points of Excellence.",
  "Harrison Weir founded the National Cat Club in 1887, the first cat registry, and became its first president.",
  "The first major American cat show was held at Madison Square Garden in New York in 1895.",
  "The Cat Fanciers' Association, or CFA, was founded in the United States in 1906.",
  "The Governing Council of the Cat Fancy (GCCF) was formed in Britain in 1910 and remains the main UK registry.",
  "FIFe, the Federation Internationale Feline, was founded in 1949 and unites cat clubs from many countries.",
  "The International Cat Association (TICA) was founded in 1979 in the United States and calls itself a genetic registry.",
  "At a cat show each cat is judged against a written breed standard, not directly against the other cats present.",
  "A breed standard describes the ideal cat of a breed and gives points for each feature, usually adding up to 100.",
  "A pedigree is a cat's family tree listing its registered ancestors, while a breed standard describes the ideal cat.",
  "Many cat shows have a household pet class, where non-pedigree cats are judged on condition and temperament.",
  "Domestic shorthair and domestic longhair are the terms for non-pedigree cats, sorted only by coat length.",
  "Moggy is the British word for a cat of no particular breed.",
  "Pedigree cats are a minority: by most estimates only a few percent of pet cats are pedigreed, and the rest are moggies.",
  "The CFA recognises about 45 breeds and TICA more than 70, and the numbers change as new breeds are accepted.",
  "Show titles such as Champion and Grand Champion are earned by winning a set number of awards under different judges.",
  "Cat fanciers call a breeding female a queen, a breeding male a stud, and a kitten's parents its dam and sire.",
  "FIFe and the GCCF do not register the Scottish Fold, while the CFA and TICA do.",
  "The Persian, Siamese and Manx were all among the breeds shown at the very first cat show in 1871.",
  "In recent years the most registered breed has been the Ragdoll in the CFA and the British Shorthair in the GCCF.",
  "The word cobby, used in breed standards, means a short, compact, heavy-boned body like the Persian's.",
  "The classic Abyssinian colour is called ruddy in North America and usual in Britain.",
  "American Bobtail tails vary in length and shape from cat to cat, often with kinks and bumps.",
  "The American Curl comes in longhair and shorthair varieties and is accepted in every colour and pattern.",
  "Apart from its coat, the American Wirehair is judged to much the same standard as the American Shorthair it came from.",
  "The Australian Mist's spots or marbling seem to sit on a misty background because the coat hairs are ticked.",
  "Some Bengals have a glitter coat, where translucent hair tips make the coat sparkle in the light.",
  "The Birman nearly died out in Europe during the Second World War, and the breed was rebuilt from a handful of cats.",
  "Many Birman breeders follow a French custom: every kitten born in one year gets a name starting with the same letter.",
  "The British Shorthair's thick plush coat is described as crisp in the standard and stands away from the body.",
  "British Shorthairs have a round face with full cheeks, a stocky body and a calm, easygoing nature.",
  "British and American Burmese differ in type: the American cat is rounder, the British more moderate and slender.",
  "The Burmilla was first recognised in Britain, and it gained championship status in the CFA in 2014.",
  "The Chartreux has a dense double coat with a woolly texture that breaks open like a sheep's fleece when the cat moves.",
  "The Cornish Rex coat gene is recessive, so two Cornish Rex parents produce only curly-coated kittens.",
  "Donskoy cats come in several coat types, from fully hairless rubber bald to short velour or wiry brush coats.",
  "The Egyptian Mau is shown in the CFA in three colours: silver, bronze and smoke.",
  "The Egyptian Mau has a flap of loose skin from the flank to the back knee, which lets it lengthen its stride.",
  "Egyptian Maus have light green eyes, described as gooseberry green, which may take over a year to develop fully.",
  "Because the longhair gene is recessive, some Exotic Shorthair litters include longhaired kittens.",
  "In Britain the Havana became part of the Oriental Shorthair, while American cats kept the Havana Brown name.",
  "The Himalayan was developed in the 1930s to 1950s by breeders in the United States and Britain.",
  "A Japanese Bobtail's hind legs are longer than its front legs, but they bend so the back stays level when it stands.",
  "The first Korats in the West arrived in the United States in 1959 as a gift to an American breeder.",
  "The LaPerm's name refers to the hair perm, because its curls look like a permanent wave.",
  "TICA granted the Lykoi championship status in 2017.",
  "Lykoi cats moult heavily at times and can become almost bald, then grow the coat back again.",
  "The Maine Coon is sometimes called the gentle giant for its size and friendly nature.",
  "Many Maine Coons have polydactyl ancestors, and some registries now accept extra-toed Maine Coons.",
  "The Manx standard calls for roundness everywhere: a round head, round eyes, round cheeks and a rounded rump.",
  "Short-legged cats were reported in Britain in the 1940s and in Russia in the 1950s, before the Munchkin breed existed.",
  "The Norwegian Forest Cat nearly vanished as a distinct type in the Second World War and was rebuilt in the 1970s.",
  "The Ocicat is accepted in twelve colours: tawny, chocolate, cinnamon, blue, lavender and fawn, each also in silver.",
  "The Oriental Shorthair was accepted for championship by the CFA in 1977.",
  "Traditional or doll-face Persians have the older, less flattened look, and some breeders specialise in them.",
  "The Chinchilla is a silver-tipped Persian with green eyes, treated as a separate breed by the GCCF in Britain.",
  "Peterbalds have long oval toes with webbing between them, which lets them pick up and hold small objects.",
  "Ragamuffins are large, plush cats with a sweet expression and are known for a docile, relaxed temperament.",
  "Ann Baker trademarked the Ragdoll name and set up her own registry, which split the breed's early breeders.",
  "The Russian Blue's double coat stands out from the body, and a pattern traced in it with a finger stays until smoothed.",
  "The Russian Blue is often said to have a slight upturn at the corners of the mouth that gives it a Mona Lisa smile.",
  "The condition linked to the Scottish Fold gene is called osteochondrodysplasia, which affects cartilage and bone growth.",
  "In Scotland the breed was first called the lop-eared cat, after lop-eared rabbits, before it became the Scottish Fold.",
  "The Selkirk Rex comes in longhair and shorthair varieties, and both have curly whiskers.",
  "Selkirk Rex kittens are born curly, and the coat straightens for a time before the curls return at 8 to 10 months.",
  "Modern show Siamese have a wedge-shaped head, large ears and a long tubular body, far slimmer than cats of the 1950s.",
  "Singapore has used the Singapura as a tourism mascot under the name Kucinta, meaning love cat.",
  "The modern Sphynx descends mainly from hairless kittens found in Minnesota and Toronto in the 1970s, not from Prune.",
  "The Toyger standard asks for bold, branching vertical stripes like a tiger's, with circular markings on the head.",
  "The Turkish Angora has a fine silky single coat with no woolly undercoat, and its hair is longest on the tail and ruff.",
  "The Turkish Van's coat is often compared to cashmere, and it has no woolly undercoat, so it dries quickly after a swim.",
  "The first Ukrainian Levkoy, named Levkoy Primero, was born in Kyiv in 2004.",
  "Harrison Weir published Our Cats and All About Them in 1889, the first major book on cat breeds and showing.",
  "At most North American shows the judge handles each cat on a table in a ring while the owner watches from the audience.",
  "At most GCCF shows in Britain cats are penned with plain white blankets so that judges see only the cat.",
  "Cat shows have classes for kittens, adults and neuters, so that neutered pets can compete for titles of their own.",
  "A cat registry records births and pedigrees and sets the rules for showing, while affiliated clubs run the shows.",
  "New breeds pass through trial stages, such as TICA's new breed classes, before they can compete for championships.",
  "The term cat fancy means the hobby of breeding and showing pedigree cats, and its followers are called cat fanciers.",
  "Natural breeds are those that developed on their own in one region, such as the Maine Coon, Siberian and Korat.",
  "Hybrid breeds such as the Bengal and Savannah descend partly from small wild cats, and some registries refuse them.",
  "Judges handle every cat, feeling the bone structure and coat, because much of a standard cannot be judged by eye alone.",
  "A cattery name, registered with a registry, is the prefix used in the names of all the kittens a breeder produces.",
  "The Siamese, Persian and Maine Coon are among the oldest recognised breeds, and the Lykoi and Toyger among the newest.",
  "At TICA shows a cat competes within its breed and then, in allbreed rings, against every other breed for best cat.",
  "In the show ring a disqualifying fault, such as a kinked tail in some breeds, means a cat cannot win an award.",
}

-- Coats, colours, genetics and records (245)
add {
  "The tabby is a coat pattern, not a breed, and it is the most common coat pattern among domestic cats.",
  "There are four basic tabby patterns: mackerel, classic, spotted and ticked.",
  "A mackerel tabby has narrow stripes running down its sides like fish bones, which is how the pattern got its name.",
  "The classic tabby, also called blotched, has wide swirls on its sides that often form a bullseye shape.",
  "A spotted tabby has spots along its sides instead of stripes. The spots are thought to be broken-up stripes.",
  "A ticked tabby has bands of colour on each hair but few or no stripes on its body, giving a salt-and-pepper look.",
  "The ticked pattern is dominant over the other tabby patterns, and mackerel is dominant over classic.",
  "Nearly every tabby has an M-shaped mark on its forehead, formed by the stripes above the eyes.",
  "One legend says the M on a tabby's forehead was left by Mary, who stroked a cat that warmed the infant Jesus.",
  "Another legend credits the tabby's M to the Prophet Muhammad, who was said to be very fond of his cat Muezza.",
  "A third legend says the tabby's M stands for Mau, the ancient Egyptian word for cat.",
  "The word tabby comes from a striped silk cloth once made in the Attabiyah district of Baghdad.",
  "The tabby pattern is the wild type, meaning it is the natural coat of the cat's ancestor, the African wildcat.",
  "Every cat is a tabby underneath: even a solid black cat carries a tabby pattern that is simply hidden.",
  "The agouti gene lets a tabby's pattern show by giving each hair in the pale areas bands of light and dark colour.",
  "A cat with two copies of the non-agouti gene looks solid because the pale bands on its hairs fill in with dark pigment.",
  "A deletion of just two DNA letters in the agouti gene is what turns a striped cat into a solid-coloured one.",
  "Faint tabby stripes, called ghost markings, can often be seen on a solid black cat in bright sunlight.",
  "The classic tabby pattern is recessive: a cat needs two copies of the blotched gene to show the swirls.",
  "The gene behind the classic tabby swirls was identified in 2012 and is called Taqpep.",
  "Orange cats are always tabbies: the orange gene stops the solid-colour gene from hiding the stripes.",
  "Tabby stripe patterns vary from cat to cat, and no two tabbies are marked exactly alike.",
  "A so-called brown tabby is genetically a black cat. The warm brown comes from the pale bands on its agouti hairs.",
  "A tortoiseshell cat has a coat of mixed black and orange patches, usually with little or no white.",
  "A calico cat is a tortoiseshell with white: patches of black and orange on a white background.",
  "In Britain a calico is usually called a tortoiseshell-and-white, and in Japan it is a mi-ke, meaning three fur.",
  "The gene for orange fur sits on the X chromosome, so it is inherited differently by males and females.",
  "Female cats have two X chromosomes, so one can carry orange and the other black, which produces tortoiseshell patches.",
  "Early in a female embryo, each cell switches off one of its two X chromosomes at random: this is X-inactivation.",
  "X-inactivation is why a tortoiseshell's orange and black patches are scattered randomly across her coat.",
  "A male cat has only one X chromosome, so he is normally either orange or not orange, but not both.",
  "Roughly 1 in 3,000 tortoiseshell or calico cats is male.",
  "Most male tortoiseshell cats have an extra X chromosome (XXY), similar to Klinefelter syndrome in humans.",
  "Male calico cats with XXY chromosomes are almost always sterile.",
  "A male tortoiseshell can also arise as a chimera, when two embryos fuse into one cat early in development.",
  "Both parents of a tortoiseshell kitten must between them carry the orange and the non-orange versions of the gene.",
  "A dilute tortoiseshell, with patches of blue-grey and cream instead of black and orange, is called a blue-cream.",
  "Torbie is the nickname for a tortoiseshell cat whose coloured patches also show tabby stripes.",
  "A calico whose patches show tabby stripes is sometimes called a caliby.",
  "Tortoiseshell cats often have mottled noses and paw pads, with pink and dark areas matching the patches on their fur.",
  "White spotting tends to give a calico larger, more sharply defined patches than a tortoiseshell with no white has.",
  "The first cloned cat, CC, short for CopyCat, was born in December 2001 at a university in Texas.",
  "CC did not match her calico donor: X-inactivation is random, so the clone came out grey tabby and white with no orange.",
  "The gene that makes cats orange, ARHGAP36, was pinned down by two research teams whose findings were published in 2025.",
  "Roughly 80 percent of orange cats are male, because a male needs only one copy of the X-linked orange gene.",
  "A female orange cat needs an orange gene from both parents, so her father must be orange.",
  "A male kitten's only X chromosome comes from his mother, so an orange male's mother is always orange or tortoiseshell.",
  "In the show world orange cats are called red, while everyday names include ginger and marmalade.",
  "Orange cats often develop small dark freckles, called lentigo, on their lips, nose, gums and eyelids.",
  "Orange fur is made of phaeomelanin, the same pigment that gives people red hair.",
  "A cat with the dominant white gene is white all over, no matter what colour genes it carries underneath.",
  "The dominant white gene works by stopping pigment cells from reaching the skin while the kitten develops in the womb.",
  "A white cat with the dominant white gene may be a hidden tortoiseshell, orange or tabby, and can pass those colours on.",
  "Two white cats can have coloured kittens if each parent carries only one copy of the dominant white gene.",
  "Only about 5 percent of cats are white, and most white cats can hear normally.",
  "White cats with blue eyes are far more likely to be deaf than white cats with green, gold or copper eyes.",
  "Around 65 to 85 percent of white cats with two blue eyes are deaf.",
  "Around 17 to 22 percent of white cats with eyes of a colour other than blue are deaf.",
  "A white cat with one blue eye and one of another colour is often deaf only on the side with the blue eye.",
  "White fur, blue eyes and deafness are linked because pigment cells are needed to keep the inner ear working.",
  "A deaf white kitten's hearing organ is not missing at birth. It degenerates during the first few weeks of life.",
  "Many white kittens are born with a small smudge of colour on the head that fades away as they grow.",
  "White cats and albino cats are different: a true albino has no pigment at all and very pale pinkish-blue eyes.",
  "True albino cats are very rare. They carry two copies of a recessive form of the gene behind the Siamese points.",
  "The Siamese colourpoint pattern is a form of partial albinism: pigment is only made in the cooler parts of the body.",
  "The colourpoint gene is a heat-sensitive version of tyrosinase, the enzyme that makes the pigment melanin.",
  "Siamese kittens are born almost pure white because the womb is warm all over, so no pigment forms before birth.",
  "A Siamese cat's points, the ears, face, paws and tail, darken within a few weeks of birth as those parts cool down.",
  "Colourpoint cats living in cold places tend to develop darker bodies than those living in warm homes.",
  "If a patch of a Siamese cat's fur is shaved, the fur can grow back darker because the bare skin was cooler.",
  "Colourpoint cats tend to darken with age, so an old Siamese often has far less contrast than a young one.",
  "A cat needs two copies of the colourpoint gene to have Siamese-style points. A cat with one copy looks normal.",
  "A seal point Siamese is genetically a black cat whose colour shows only on the cooler points.",
  "The four traditional Siamese point colours are seal, blue, chocolate and lilac.",
  "A lynx point cat is a colourpoint whose points show tabby stripes.",
  "Cats with the colourpoint gene always have blue eyes, because the same gene reduces pigment in the iris.",
  "Many Siamese cats have a slight squint, linked to the colourpoint gene affecting how the nerves from the eyes are wired.",
  "The Burmese sepia gene is a milder version of the same mutation: the body is only slightly paler than the points.",
  "A cat with one Siamese gene and one Burmese gene has a coat halfway between the two, a pattern called mink.",
  "Tuxedo cats are black with white on the chest, paws and belly, a look caused by the white spotting gene.",
  "The white spotting gene, also called piebald, is different from the dominant white gene that makes a cat entirely white.",
  "Dominant white and white spotting in cats are both caused by different changes in a single gene called KIT.",
  "White spots usually appear on the parts of the body that pigment cells reach last: the belly, chest, paws and chin.",
  "A locket is a small spot of white on the chest of an otherwise solid-coloured cat.",
  "White mittens, white toes and a white chin are the lowest grades of white spotting.",
  "Breeders grade white spotting on a scale of 1 to 10, from a tiny locket to an almost entirely white cat.",
  "A cat that is mostly white with colour only on the head and tail is said to have the van pattern.",
  "A harlequin cat is mostly white with several large patches of colour on the body.",
  "White spotting is dominant: one copy is enough to produce white patches, though the amount of white varies a lot.",
  "A smoke cat looks solid-coloured at rest, but its hairs are white at the root, so the coat flashes pale when it moves.",
  "Smoke, shaded and chinchilla coats come from the inhibitor gene, which blocks pigment on the lower part of each hair.",
  "The inhibitor gene is dominant, so one copy is enough to make a silver tabby, a smoke or a shaded cat.",
  "A chinchilla cat, also called shell or tipped, has pigment only on the very tip of each hair, giving a sparkling look.",
  "In show terms a chinchilla has colour on about the top eighth of each hair, and a shaded cat on about the top third.",
  "A silver tabby is a tabby whose pale background hairs have been turned white by the inhibitor gene.",
  "The recessive dilute gene lightens colour: black becomes blue, chocolate becomes lilac and orange becomes cream.",
  "A blue cat is really grey: in cat fancy terms, blue is the name for the dilute form of black.",
  "Old cat books call blue cats Maltese, a name still heard in some places.",
  "Dilution works by clumping the pigment granules in each hair unevenly, so the colour looks paler.",
  "The gene behind dilute colour is melanophilin (MLPH), and a cat needs two copies of the dilute version to be blue.",
  "Two black cats can have blue kittens if both parents carry a hidden copy of the recessive dilute gene.",
  "Lilac, also called lavender, is the dilute form of chocolate, and fawn is the dilute form of cinnamon.",
  "Cream is the dilute form of orange, so a cream cat carries the orange gene plus two copies of the dilute gene.",
  "Chocolate and cinnamon fur come from two different recessive versions of the gene TYRP1, which alters black pigment.",
  "The sorrel colour of the Abyssinian is genetically cinnamon, a light reddish-brown form of black pigment.",
  "Amber, a rare colour seen in Norwegian Forest Cats, comes from a change in the gene MC1R: black kittens turn golden.",
  "Genetically, every cat colour is a variation on black or orange, plus white where pigment is missing.",
  "There is no gene for white pigment: white fur is simply hair with no pigment in it.",
  "A cat's coat colours come from two pigments: eumelanin for black and brown, and phaeomelanin for red and yellow.",
  "A cat's nose and paw pad colour usually follows its coat genes: black cats have black pads and orange cats pink ones.",
  "Eye colour comes from melanin in the iris: a lot gives copper or gold, less gives green, and almost none gives blue.",
  "All kittens are born with blue eyes, because the iris has not yet produced its pigment.",
  "A kitten's adult eye colour starts to show at around six or seven weeks and is usually settled by three to four months.",
  "Blue eyes in cats contain no blue pigment: they look blue because light scatters in an iris with little or no melanin.",
  "Copper is the darkest eye colour that cats normally have.",
  "Odd-eyed cats have one blue eye and one green, yellow or copper eye, a condition called heterochromia.",
  "Odd eyes are most common in white cats and cats with lots of white, because those genes keep pigment out of one eye.",
  "Odd-eyed cats start life with two blue eyes. One eye then gains colour over a few months while the other stays blue.",
  "An odd-eyed cat sees equally well with both eyes, as the difference in colour does not affect its vision.",
  "Some cats have two colours within a single iris, a rarer form of heterochromia called sectoral or dichroic.",
  "Eye colour is mostly independent of coat colour, with exceptions such as white cats and colourpoints.",
  "Polydactyly means having extra toes. A normal cat has 18, five on each front paw and four on each back paw.",
  "Polydactyly is usually caused by a dominant gene, so a polydactyl parent often passes extra toes to many of its kittens.",
  "Polydactyl cats are especially common along the east coast of North America and in south-west England and Wales.",
  "Polydactyly is thought to have spread with ships, since sailors valued the big-pawed cats as mousers and lucky charms.",
  "The Ernest Hemingway Home in Key West, Florida, keeps around 60 cats, about half of them polydactyl.",
  "Hemingway's first polydactyl cat, Snow White, was reportedly given to him by a ship's captain.",
  "Polydactyl cats are nicknamed Hemingway cats, mitten cats and thumb cats.",
  "The most toes on a cat on record is 28: Jake, a ginger tabby from Ontario, Canada, had seven toes on each paw.",
  "Polydactyly usually affects the front paws, and extra toes on the back paws alone are rare.",
  "The change behind the Hemingway cats' extra toes lies in a control region of a gene called Sonic hedgehog.",
  "Many early Maine Coons were polydactyl, but extra toes were later barred from the show ring for the breed.",
  "The Manx's missing tail comes from a dominant gene that shortens the spine. Kittens with two copies die before birth.",
  "Because the Manx gene is lethal in a double dose, every tailless Manx has one tailless gene and one normal gene.",
  "Manx cats come in rumpy (no tail), rumpy riser (a small bump), stumpy (a short tail) and longy (a near-full tail).",
  "The Manx mutation lies in a gene called T, or brachyury, which guides how the spine forms in the embryo.",
  "A folk tale says the Manx lost its tail when Noah shut the Ark door on it. In reality a gene mutation is responsible.",
  "The Japanese Bobtail's pom-pom tail comes from a recessive gene, unlike the dominant Manx gene, and is harmless.",
  "The gene behind the Japanese Bobtail's kinked tail, HES7, was identified in 2016. It controls how vertebrae form.",
  "No two Japanese Bobtail tails are alike: the kinks and curves differ from cat to cat.",
  "Kinked and bobbed tails are common among street cats in Japan and much of Southeast Asia.",
  "Long hair in cats is recessive: a longhaired kitten needs a long-hair gene from both parents.",
  "Two shorthaired cats can produce a longhaired kitten if both carry the hidden long-hair gene.",
  "A longhaired cat has two copies of the long-hair gene, so every one of its kittens carries at least one copy.",
  "Long fur in cats comes from changes in the gene FGF5, which also controls hair length in dogs and other mammals.",
  "At least four different mutations in FGF5 cause long hair in cat breeds, and all of them have the same effect.",
  "FGF5 normally tells a hair to stop growing. When the gene is broken, each hair keeps growing longer before it is shed.",
  "The Sphynx's hairlessness comes from a recessive change in the gene KRT71, which makes a protein in the hair shaft.",
  "A different change in the same KRT71 gene gives the Devon Rex its curly coat, and a third curls the Selkirk Rex.",
  "Sphynx cats are not truly hairless: they are covered in a fine down that feels like warm suede or chamois leather.",
  "The Sphynx breed traces back to a hairless kitten called Prune, born in Toronto in 1966.",
  "The Donskoy, a hairless cat from Russia, owes its bare skin to a dominant gene, unlike the recessive gene of the Sphynx.",
  "The Donskoy breed began with a kitten called Varvara, found in Rostov-on-Don in 1987.",
  "Hairless cats still have colour genes: their skin shows the pattern the fur would have had, including tabby stripes.",
  "Sphynx cats often have few whiskers, or whiskers that are short and broken.",
  "The Lykoi, or werewolf cat, has a sparse, patchy coat caused by recessive changes in the gene HR.",
  "Rex cats are named after Rex rabbits, which have a similar plush, wavy coat.",
  "The Cornish Rex has no guard hairs at all, only the soft undercoat hairs, which lie in tight waves.",
  "The Cornish Rex gene is recessive and first appeared in a kitten called Kallibunker, born in Cornwall in 1950.",
  "The Cornish Rex curl comes from a change in the gene LPAR6, identified in 2013.",
  "The Devon Rex arose from a separate recessive mutation found in Devon in 1960, a different gene from the Cornish Rex.",
  "Devon Rex whiskers are short and brittle and often break off.",
  "The Selkirk Rex's curl is dominant, so a kitten needs only one curly parent to inherit the wavy coat.",
  "The LaPerm's curls come from a dominant gene. The first LaPerm was born bald in Oregon in 1982 and grew curls later.",
  "The American Wirehair's springy coat comes from a dominant mutation found in a litter in upstate New York in 1966.",
  "A fever coat happens when a pregnant cat has a fever or severe stress, so her kittens are born with pale, silvery fur.",
  "Fever coat kittens grow out of it: their true colour comes in as the pale fur sheds over the first months.",
  "A fever coat is not inherited. It is thought to be caused by heat disturbing pigment while the kittens are in the womb.",
  "Black cats can rust: hours in the sun can turn a black coat reddish brown as sunlight breaks down the pigment.",
  "Black fur that looks rusty can also mean a diet short of the amino acid tyrosine, which cats use to make dark pigment.",
  "Kittens in one litter can look nothing alike because each one gets a random half of each parent's genes.",
  "A female cat can mate with several males during one heat, so a single litter can have more than one father.",
  "The domestic cat has 38 chromosomes, arranged in 19 pairs, while humans have 46 and dogs have 78.",
  "The cat genome was first sequenced in 2007 from an Abyssinian cat named Cinnamon.",
  "The cat genome contains roughly 20,000 protein-coding genes, a similar number to the human genome.",
  "In 2004 a Texas woman paid 50,000 dollars for Little Nicky, the first commercially cloned pet cat.",
  "A cat's coat has three kinds of hair: long guard hairs, mid-length awn hairs and the soft, short down hairs beneath.",
  "A cat's fur is dense, with roughly 10,000 to 20,000 hairs per square centimetre, thicker on the belly than on the back.",
  "Creme Puff's owner, Jake Perry, also owned Granpa Rex Allen, who held the oldest cat record before her at 34 years.",
  "Jake Perry reportedly fed his record-breaking cats a breakfast of eggs, turkey bacon, broccoli and coffee with cream.",
  "Earlier Guinness books listed a tabby called Puss from Devon, England, who was said to have died in 1939 aged 36.",
  "Flossie, a British tortoiseshell-and-white born in December 1995, was named the oldest living cat in 2022 at nearly 27.",
  "Scooter, a Siamese from Mansfield, Texas, was recognised as the oldest living cat in 2016 at the age of 30.",
  "Corduroy, a cat from Oregon born in 1989, was recognised as the oldest living cat in 2015 at the age of 26.",
  "Tiffany Two, a tortoiseshell from San Diego, held the oldest living cat title before she died in 2015 aged 27.",
  "Poppy, a cat from Bournemouth, England, was named the oldest living cat in 2014 at 24, shortly before she died.",
  "Rubble, a Maine Coon from Exeter, England, was reported to be 31 in 2019, but his age was never officially verified.",
  "The longest domestic cat on record was Stewie, a Maine Coon from Reno, Nevada, measuring 123 cm (48.5 in) nose to tail.",
  "Stewie, whose full name was Mymains Stewart Gilligan, was a certified therapy animal who visited a local senior centre.",
  "Stewie set his record in 2010 and died of cancer in 2013.",
  "Before Stewie, the longest cat record belonged to Leo, a Maine Coon from Chicago measured at 121.9 cm (48 in) in 2002.",
  "Every holder of the longest domestic cat record so far has been a Maine Coon.",
  "Barivel, a Maine Coon from Vigevano, Italy, was named the longest domestic cat living in 2018 at 120 cm (3 ft 11.2 in).",
  "Barivel's name means clown or joker in a local Italian dialect, though his owners describe him as shy and quiet.",
  "Ludo, a Maine Coon from Wakefield, England, held the longest living cat record in 2015 at 118.33 cm (3 ft 10.59 in).",
  "The tallest domestic cat on record was Arcturus, a Savannah from Michigan, who stood 48.4 cm (19.05 in) at the shoulder.",
  "Savannah cats are tall because they descend from the serval, a long-legged African wild cat.",
  "Cygnus Regulus Powers, a silver Maine Coon, had the longest tail on a domestic cat on record at 44.66 cm (17.58 in).",
  "Arcturus and Cygnus lived in the same home in Farmington Hills, Michigan, and both died in a house fire in 2017.",
  "The same family later owned Fenrir, a Savannah recognised in 2021 as the tallest living cat at 47.83 cm (18.83 in).",
  "Altair, a silver Maine Coon from Michigan, was recognised in 2021 for the longest tail on a living cat, 40.7 cm.",
  "Trouble, a Savannah from Sacramento, California, held the tallest cat record in 2011 at 48.3 cm (19 in).",
  "Stewie also held a record for the longest tail on a domestic cat, 41.5 cm (16.34 in), before Cygnus beat it.",
  "The heaviest cat on record, Himmy, a tabby from Cairns, Australia, weighed 21.3 kg (46 lb 15 oz) when he died in 1986.",
  "Himmy's waist measured 84 cm (33 in), and his owner had to carry him around in a wheelbarrow.",
  "Guinness World Records retired its heaviest pet categories in the late 1990s so owners would not overfeed pets to win.",
  "An 18 kg cat called Meow drew headlines in 2012, but Guinness no longer lists heaviest cats, so no record was set.",
  "The smallest cat on record was Tinker Toy, a Himalayan-Persian from Illinois, just 7 cm (2.75 in) tall fully grown.",
  "Tinker Toy measured 19 cm (7.5 in) long. He was the runt of a litter of six and lived from 1990 to 1997.",
  "Lilieput, a Munchkin from Napa, California, was recognised in 2013 as the shortest living cat at 13.34 cm (5.25 in).",
  "Fizz Girl, a Munchkin from San Diego, held the shortest living cat record in 2010 at 15.24 cm (6 in) tall.",
  "The loudest purr by a domestic cat on record is 67.8 decibels, by Merlin, a rescue cat from Torquay, England, in 2015.",
  "Merlin's purr was about as loud as a dishwasher, while a typical cat purrs at around 25 decibels.",
  "Before Merlin, the loudest purr on record was 67.68 decibels, by Smokey from Northamptonshire, England, in 2011.",
  "In 2023 Guinness recognised Bella, from Huntingdon, England, for the loudest purr by a living cat at 54.59 decibels.",
  "The most prolific cat on record was Dusty, a tabby from Bonham, Texas, who produced 420 kittens in her lifetime.",
  "Dusty's last litter, a single kitten, was born in June 1952.",
  "The largest litter on record was 19 kittens, born on 7 August 1970 to a Burmese-Siamese cat in Oxfordshire, England.",
  "Of the record litter of 19 kittens born in 1970, four were stillborn and 15 survived.",
  "A typical cat litter has three to five kittens, so a litter of 19 is about four times the usual size.",
  "The oldest cat on record to give birth was Kitty, from Staffordshire, England, who had two kittens in 1987 aged 30.",
  "The longest cat whiskers on record measured 19 cm (7.5 in) and belonged to Missi, a Maine Coon from Finland, in 2005.",
  "Colonel Meow, a Himalayan-Persian cross from Los Angeles, held the longest fur record in 2013 with hairs of 22.87 cm.",
  "The longest fur on a cat on record is 25.68 cm (10.11 in), measured on Sophie Smith of Oceanside, California, in 2013.",
  "Alley, a rescue cat from Austin, Texas, set a record for the longest jump by a cat in 2013 at 182.88 cm (6 ft).",
  "Waffle the Warrior Cat, from Big Sur, California, raised the longest jump record to 213.36 cm (7 ft) in 2018.",
  "The most tricks by a cat in one minute on record is 26, by Alexis and her trainer Anika Moritz in Austria in 2020.",
  "Didga, a cat from Tweed Heads, Australia, set a record of 24 tricks in one minute in 2016, including a skateboard jump.",
  "The fastest domestic cat breed on record is the Egyptian Mau, clocked at around 48 km/h (30 mph).",
  "A healthy domestic cat can sprint at around 48 km/h (30 mph), faster than Usain Bolt's top speed of about 45 km/h.",
  "Sugar, a cat from Boston, survived a fall of 19 floors in 2012, landing on a patch of mulch with only minor injuries.",
  "A 1987 study of 132 cats that fell from New York high-rises found 90 percent survived. It named high-rise syndrome.",
  "The 1987 study found cats that fell from seven or more storeys had fewer injuries than those that fell from two to six.",
  "A falling cat reaches its top speed, about 100 km/h (60 mph), after roughly five storeys, then relaxes and spreads out.",
  "Blackie, a British cat, inherited 7 million pounds when his owner Ben Rea died in 1988, the wealthiest cat on record.",
  "Ben Rea left his fortune to Blackie, the last of his 15 cats, and to three cat charities, leaving out his family.",
  "Karl Lagerfeld said his Birman cat Choupette would be one of his heirs. She reportedly earned 3 million euros in 2014.",
  "A former stray named Tommaso in Rome was reportedly left an estate worth around 10 million euros by his owner in 2011.",
  "Nala, a Siamese-tabby cross adopted from a Los Angeles shelter, set the record for most Instagram followers by a cat.",
  "Nala passed 4.3 million Instagram followers when her record was confirmed in 2020.",
  "The greatest mouser on record was Towser, a tortoiseshell who caught an estimated 28,899 mice at a Scottish distillery.",
  "Towser's tally was worked out by observers who counted her catches over several days and scaled up over her 24 years.",
  "Towser averaged roughly three mice a day, and a bronze statue of her stands at the Glenturret distillery in Scotland.",
  "Holly, a cat from West Palm Beach, Florida, walked about 300 km (190 miles) home after getting lost at Daytona Beach.",
  "Holly was identified by her microchip when she turned up near home in early 2013, two months after she vanished.",
}

-- Cats through history (231)
add {
  "The domestic cat descends from the Near Eastern wildcat, Felis lybica, not from the European wildcat.",
  "Cats were first drawn to people by the mice that gathered around the earliest grain stores of the Fertile Crescent.",
  "The partnership between cats and farmers began roughly 10,000 years ago in the Near East.",
  "A 9,500-year-old grave on Cyprus holds a person buried beside a cat, the oldest known sign of cats living with people.",
  "Cyprus has no native wildcats, so the cat in that ancient grave must have been carried to the island by boat.",
  "A 2017 DNA study found that cats spread from the Near East and later from Egypt in two separate waves.",
  "Cats are often said to have domesticated themselves, choosing to live near people for the easy hunting.",
  "The cat genome has changed very little since domestication, far less than the dog's has.",
  "For most of their history cats were not bred for jobs. They simply hunted the vermin that lived around people.",
  "Early farmers gained a pest controller and cats gained a steady supply of mice, a bargain that suited both sides.",
  "Bones from a 5,300-year-old village in China show that local leopard cats moved in to hunt the farmers' mice.",
  "Wild Felis lybica still live across North Africa and the Near East, and they look much like a lean tabby house cat.",
  "Bones of Near Eastern wildcats found in Poland show cats had followed farmers into central Europe about 6,000 years ago.",
  "At Hierakonpolis in Egypt, six cats buried together about 5,700 years ago may be an early sign of cat keeping there.",
  "The Egyptian goddess Bastet was shown first as a lioness and later, from about 1000 BC, as a cat or cat-headed woman.",
  "Bastet's main temple stood at Bubastis in the Nile Delta, where Herodotus described a great annual festival.",
  "Herodotus wrote that more wine was drunk at the Bubastis festival than in all the rest of the year.",
  "Hundreds of thousands of cat mummies were buried in Egyptian cat cemeteries such as the ones at Bubastis and Beni Hasan.",
  "In 1890 a shipment of about 180,000 Egyptian cat mummies was sent to Liverpool and auctioned off as fertilizer.",
  "Many Egyptian cat mummies were young cats killed on purpose to be sold to pilgrims as offerings to Bastet.",
  "Herodotus wrote that when a house cat died in Egypt, the whole household shaved their eyebrows in mourning.",
  "Herodotus wrote that Egyptians at a fire cared more about saving the cats than about putting out the flames.",
  "Herodotus reported that dead cats were carried to Bubastis to be embalmed and buried in sacred vaults.",
  "The Greek writer Diodorus Siculus said that killing a cat in Egypt, even by accident, could be punished by death.",
  "Diodorus Siculus recorded that a Roman who accidentally killed a cat in Egypt was killed by an angry Egyptian crowd.",
  "Ancient Egypt is said to have banned the export of cats, though cats still spread abroad through trade.",
  "A story told by the Greek writer Polyaenus claims the Persians carried cats into the Battle of Pelusium in 525 BC.",
  "Legend says Egyptian soldiers at Pelusium in 525 BC would not strike at Persians who held cats in front of them.",
  "Egyptian tomb art shows cats with people from the Middle Kingdom onward, about 4,000 years ago.",
  "In Egyptian tomb paintings of the New Kingdom, cats often sit under the chair of the lady of the house.",
  "A tomb painting of Nebamun from about 1350 BC shows a cat catching birds in the marshes beside its owner's family.",
  "The ancient Egyptian word for cat was miu or miw, which seems to copy the sound of a meow.",
  "Prince Thutmose, a son of Amenhotep III, had his cat buried in a stone coffin carved with funeral spells.",
  "Egyptian craftsmen made bronze cat statues with gold earrings and nose rings as offerings to Bastet.",
  "Bastet was linked with the home, fertility and the protection of the household, and cats were her sacred animals.",
  "The Egyptian sun god Ra was sometimes shown as a great cat cutting off the head of the serpent Apophis.",
  "Herodotus visited Egypt in the 5th century BC and is the main source for many of the stories about Egyptian cats.",
  "Before Bastet, the Egyptian goddess Mafdet was shown as a cat-like protector against snakes and scorpions.",
  "Ancient Egyptian satirical drawings show a topsy-turvy world in which cats wait on mice and herd geese.",
  "In 2018 archaeologists found dozens of cat mummies in newly opened tombs at Saqqara, near Cairo.",
  "The ancient Greeks mostly kept ferrets and weasels, not cats, to deal with mice in their homes and stores.",
  "Cats appear only rarely in classical Greek art, usually as exotic pets rather than working mousers.",
  "Phoenician traders are credited with carrying cats from Egypt around the Mediterranean in their ships.",
  "The Greek word for cat, ailouros, meant something like waving tail, after the animal's restless tail.",
  "Cats spread through Europe with the Roman army, which valued them for protecting its grain stores from rodents.",
  "The oldest cat remains found in Britain date from the Iron Age, before the Roman conquest of the island.",
  "Romans kept cats, and cat paw prints have been found pressed into Roman roof tiles that were left to dry outdoors.",
  "A famous mosaic from Pompeii shows a tabby cat pouncing on a bird, and it is now in the museum in Naples.",
  "Cat bones have been found at many Roman forts and villas across Britain and the rest of the empire.",
  "The Latin word cattus, the root of the English word cat, only appeared in writing in the 4th century AD.",
  "Before cattus, the Romans used the word feles, which could mean a cat or another small hunter such as a marten.",
  "In Norse myth the goddess Freyja rode in a chariot pulled by two cats, as told in the 13th-century Prose Edda.",
  "Vikings carried cats on their longships to keep down rats and mice on long voyages and in new settlements.",
  "A cat found at a Viking trading port on Germany's Baltic coast carried DNA from the Egyptian cat lineage.",
  "Norse settlers brought cats with them to Iceland in the 9th century, and the island had no cats before that.",
  "Cat skins were a trade good in the Viking Age, and bones from Danish sites show some cats were skinned for fur.",
  "A 2018 study of cat bones from Denmark found that cats have grown about 16 percent larger since the Viking Age.",
  "In ancient China cats were valued for protecting silkworm cocoons and grain stores from rats and mice.",
  "The Chinese Book of Rites, compiled over 2,000 years ago, mentions a year-end offering to cats for eating field rats.",
  "The Chinese word for cat, mao, sounds like the animal's own call, as the Egyptian word miu did.",
  "Paintings from China's Song dynasty, about 1,000 years ago, show cats kept as pampered household pets.",
  "Tradition says cats came to Japan from China with Buddhist scriptures, kept aboard ship to guard the scrolls from mice.",
  "Cat bones found on a Japanese island date to the Yayoi period, about 2,000 years ago, earlier than the old stories said.",
  "Japan's Emperor Uda wrote in his diary in 889 about a black cat given to him, praising its glossy coat and grace.",
  "In Heian Japan, around the year 1000, cats were rare luxury pets of the court and were sometimes kept on leashes.",
  "The Pillow Book records that a cat of Japan's Emperor Ichijo was given the rank of a court lady around the year 1000.",
  "In 1602 the rulers of Kyoto ordered cats untied and let roam to control rats that threatened the silk industry.",
  "In Edo-period Japan, when cats were scarce, farmers bought pictures of cats as charms to keep mice from silkworms.",
  "Legend says the beckoning cat figure began at Gotokuji temple in Tokyo, where a cat waved a lord in out of a storm.",
  "Gotokuji temple in Tokyo today displays thousands of beckoning cat figures left by grateful visitors.",
  "The 19th-century Japanese print artist Utagawa Kuniyoshi loved cats and worked them into many of his woodblock prints.",
  "Several small Japanese islands, such as Aoshima, have far more cats than people, descended from cats kept by fishermen.",
  "Thai manuscripts known as the Cat Book Poems, written centuries ago, describe which cats bring good or bad luck.",
  "Cats are regarded as clean animals in Islam and are allowed inside mosques, unlike dogs.",
  "The Prophet Muhammad is remembered as fond of cats, and several hadith tell of his kindness to them.",
  "A famous companion of the Prophet was known as Abu Hurairah, meaning father of the kitten, because he kept a cat.",
  "A hadith tells of a woman punished for locking up a cat until it starved, a lesson in kindness to animals.",
  "A popular story says the Prophet cut off his sleeve rather than wake a cat asleep on it, but it is not in the hadith.",
  "In Muslim tradition, water a cat has drunk from stays clean enough to use for washing before prayer.",
  "In 13th-century Cairo the Mamluk sultan Baybars left a garden in his will so that the city's stray cats would be fed.",
  "Edward William Lane wrote in the 1830s that cats were still fed every afternoon in the courtyard of a Cairo court.",
  "In Ottoman Istanbul, charitable foundations paid for food for stray cats and dogs, a custom that lives on today.",
  "Istanbul is famous for its hundreds of thousands of street cats, fed and sheltered by residents and the city.",
  "The 10th-century Welsh laws of Hywel Dda priced a kitten at one penny before its eyes opened and four pence as a mouser.",
  "The laws of Hywel Dda listed what a cat must have: good ears, eyes, teeth and claws, and skill at killing mice.",
  "In Welsh law, someone who killed a cat guarding the king's barn owed enough grain to bury the cat held up by its tail.",
  "Early Welsh law counted a cat among the things a hamlet needed to be complete, alongside a plough, a kiln and a churn.",
  "The 9th-century poem Pangur Ban, written by an Irish monk abroad, compares his scholarship to his white cat's hunting.",
  "In 1233 Pope Gregory IX issued the bull Vox in Rama, which described a heretical sect worshipping a black cat.",
  "Vox in Rama is often blamed for a mass killing of cats, but historians find no evidence of a church-ordered cat purge.",
  "From the late Middle Ages, cats, especially black ones, were linked in European imagination to witches and the devil.",
  "At England's first witch trial ending in execution, in 1566, the accused was said to keep a spotted cat as a familiar.",
  "The claim that medieval Europe killed so many cats that plague rats ran wild is a myth. There was no mass purge.",
  "The Black Death spread mainly through fleas, and cities had far more rats than their cats could ever have controlled.",
  "Medieval monasteries kept cats, and a 13th-century rule for anchoresses allowed them a cat but no other animal.",
  "Medieval manuscripts sometimes bear inky paw prints where a cat walked across the page as the scribe worked.",
  "A 15th-century scribe wrote a curse in his manuscript against a cat that had urinated on the page overnight.",
  "Cat skins were traded as a cheap fur in medieval Europe, lining the clothes of people who could not afford better.",
  "Exeter Cathedral's medieval accounts record regular payments to feed its cat, and an old cat door survives there.",
  "Dried cats have been found sealed inside the walls of old European buildings, probably put there to ward off evil.",
  "In Christian art a cat sometimes sits near Judas at the Last Supper, as in Ghirlandaio's fresco in Florence.",
  "Leonardo da Vinci filled a sheet with sketches of cats in many poses, studying how they moved and fought.",
  "The Belgian town of Ypres holds the Kattenstoet, a cat parade, every three years, recalling a medieval custom.",
  "Live cats were thrown from the belfry of Ypres until 1817. Today a jester throws plush toy cats to the crowd instead.",
  "In early modern France, cats were sometimes burned in Midsummer bonfires, a cruel custom recorded in Paris and Metz.",
  "In the 1730s apprentices at a Paris printing shop staged a mock trial and massacre of the neighborhood's cats.",
  "The 17th-century French statesman Cardinal Richelieu kept more than a dozen cats and left money for their care.",
  "St Gertrude of Nivelles, a 7th-century abbess, has become a patron saint of cats, though the link is a modern one.",
  "A Cyprus monastery, St Nicholas of the Cats, recalls a legend that cats were brought in the 4th century to kill snakes.",
  "In Russian Orthodox tradition cats are often the only animals allowed inside churches, valued there as mousers.",
  "Ships' cats sailed on merchant and navy ships for centuries to protect food stores, ropes and sails from rats.",
  "Sailors believed cats brought luck at sea, and a cat lost overboard was feared to be a sign of a coming storm.",
  "Before Europeans arrived, the Americas had no domestic cats. The only cats known there were wild ones.",
  "Domestic cats arrived in the Americas aboard European ships in the 16th century and spread with the colonists.",
  "During the winter of 1609 to 1610 at Jamestown, starving English colonists ate their cats and dogs.",
  "Domestic cats reached Australia on European ships in the 18th century and covered the continent within about 100 years.",
  "Domestic cats reached New Zealand on European ships in the late 18th and early 19th centuries.",
  "Cats brought to Stephens Island, New Zealand, in 1894 wiped out the island's flightless wren within a few years.",
  "Cats released from ships went wild on many islands and remain a threat to island birds that evolved without predators.",
  "Five cats taken to Marion Island in 1949 to control mice grew into thousands, and it took until 1991 to remove them.",
  "The Royal Navy allowed cats aboard its ships until 1975, when they were banned on hygiene grounds.",
  "The first major cat show was held at the Crystal Palace in London in 1871, organized by the artist Harrison Weir.",
  "Harrison Weir wrote the first standards for judging cats at shows and is often called the father of the cat fancy.",
  "Britain's National Cat Club, founded in 1887, kept the first register of pedigree cats.",
  "The first big American cat show was held at Madison Square Garden in New York in 1895.",
  "The Cat Fanciers' Association, now the largest registry of pedigree cats, was founded in the United States in 1906.",
  "The British artist Louis Wain became famous in the 1880s and 1890s for drawings of cats walking upright like people.",
  "Louis Wain served as chairman of Britain's National Cat Club and judged at cat shows.",
  "H. G. Wells said Louis Wain had invented a whole cat world, and Wain's cats appeared on postcards and in annuals.",
  "Louis Wain spent his last years in mental hospitals, still drawing cats, after a public appeal led by H. G. Wells.",
  "In the 1870s the Brighton photographer Harry Pointer made some of the first comic cat photographs, posed with captions.",
  "Britain's Post Office officially employed cats from 1868, paying a weekly allowance for their food to control mice.",
  "Britain's Post Office kept cats on its books for more than a century, with the last official one dying in 1984.",
  "Britain's government has kept cats at 10 Downing Street since at least the 1920s, with the title Chief Mouser.",
  "Victorian London had cat's meat men who went door to door selling skewers of horse meat for household cats.",
  "In Victorian Britain the cat changed from a working mouser into a household pet of the growing middle class.",
  "Spratt's, a British firm that began with dog biscuits in 1860, was among the first to sell commercial cat food.",
  "The cats of the Hermitage Museum in St Petersburg trace their line to cats Empress Elizabeth ordered from Kazan in 1745.",
  "Empress Elizabeth's 1745 decree called for the best mousing cats of Kazan to be sent to the Winter Palace.",
  "Catherine the Great is said to have given the palace cats the status of guardians of the picture galleries.",
  "The Hermitage cats died out during the siege of Leningrad, and trainloads of new cats were brought in after it ended.",
  "Today about 50 cats live in the basements of the Hermitage Museum, cared for by staff and even given a press secretary.",
  "The Hermitage holds a yearly Day of the Hermitage Cat to celebrate its resident mousers.",
  "A Russian custom lets a cat be the first to enter a new home, which is believed to bring good luck to the household.",
  "Cats lived in the trenches of the First World War, hunting the rats that plagued soldiers on both sides.",
  "At the start of the Second World War about 750,000 British pets, many of them cats, were put down on official advice.",
  "The Dickin Medal, Britain's award for animal bravery in war, has gone to only one cat, a ship's cat honored in 1949.",
  "Several American presidents have kept cats in the White House, beginning with Abraham Lincoln.",
  "In 1949 Illinois Governor Adlai Stevenson vetoed a bill to confine cats, arguing that roaming was in a cat's nature.",
  "Clay cat litter was invented in 1947 by Ed Lowe of Michigan, who offered a neighbor absorbent clay in place of ashes.",
  "Before clay litter, indoor cats used boxes of sand, ashes or garden earth.",
  "Cat litter made keeping cats indoors practical and helped the shift toward indoor cats in the late 20th century.",
  "The only cat to fly in space was launched by France in 1963 and returned safely to Earth by parachute.",
  "France's 1963 cat flight was a short suborbital hop to about 150 kilometers up, and it has never been repeated.",
  "In the 1960s the CIA tried to turn a cat into a listening device in a project nicknamed Acoustic Kitty, and it failed.",
  "By the late 20th century cats had overtaken dogs as the most numerous pet in American homes.",
  "The British charity Cats Protection was founded in 1927 to help stray and unwanted cats.",
  "The domestic cat's genome was first sequenced in 2007, using DNA from an Abyssinian.",
  "The world's first cat cafe opened in Taipei in 1998, and the idea spread to Japan in 2004 and then around the world.",
  "International Cat Day, held on 8 August, was started in 2002 by the International Fund for Animal Welfare.",
  "Italian law has protected free-roaming cats since 1991, making it illegal to kill them or break up their colonies.",
  "In 2001 Rome's city council declared the cats living among its ancient ruins part of the city's heritage.",
  "The Torre Argentina cat sanctuary in Rome shelters cats among the ruins where Julius Caesar was assassinated.",
  "Rome's volunteer cat feeders are known as gattare, and the actress Anna Magnani was one of the most famous of them.",
  "Cats have lived for generations among the ruins of Rome, including the Colosseum and the Forum, fed by local volunteers.",
  "In Hindu tradition the goddess Shashthi, a protector of children, is shown riding a cat.",
  "European travellers to Ottoman Istanbul often remarked on how kindly the Turks treated the city's cats.",
  "Turkish law protects street animals, and Istanbul's districts run feeding and veterinary programs for the city's cats.",
  "A colony of stray cats lived on Parliament Hill in Ottawa for decades, with a volunteer-built shelter, until 2013.",
  "The Hemingway home in Key West, Florida, houses dozens of cats, many descended from a cat given to the writer.",
  "New York's Algonquin Hotel has kept a resident cat in its lobby since the 1930s.",
  "Since the 1920s London's Savoy Hotel has seated a carved wooden cat as a fourteenth guest at any table of thirteen.",
  "Disneyland in California is home to a colony of feral cats that help keep down rodents, and the park looks after them.",
  "New York's corner shops are known for their bodega cats, kept to deter mice even though city rules frown on them.",
  "The Forbidden City in Beijing has long had resident cats, and Palace Museum staff feed the strays that live there now.",
  "The cat is not among the twelve animals of the Chinese zodiac, but it takes the rabbit's place in the Vietnamese one.",
  "In 18th-century Korea the painter Byeon Sang-byeok was so famous for his cats that he was nicknamed Byeon the Cat.",
  "Japan has celebrated Cat Day on 22 February since 1987, because two-two-two sounds like a cat's meow in Japanese.",
  "Kuching, a city in Malaysia, has a name that resembles the Malay word for cat and is home to a cat museum.",
  "The city of Kuching has large cat statues at its roundabouts and treats the cat as an unofficial symbol.",
  "Greek coins from Rhegion in southern Italy, struck around 400 BC, show a seated man playing with a cat.",
  "An Athenian marble relief from around 500 BC shows two men setting a cat and a dog against each other.",
  "The Greeks identified the Egyptian cat goddess Bastet with their own goddess Artemis.",
  "The ruins of Bubastis lie at Tell Basta, next to the modern Egyptian city of Zagazig.",
  "The Egyptian name of Bubastis was Per-Bast, meaning the House of Bastet.",
  "Scans of Egyptian cat mummies show that many of the cats were killed young by having their necks broken.",
  "Some Egyptian cat mummies turned out to contain only a few bones, or none at all, sold to pilgrims as the real thing.",
  "Statues of Bastet often show her holding a sistrum, a kind of rattle, and a basket, sometimes with kittens at her feet.",
  "The 2017 DNA study of cat origins used remains from more than 200 ancient cats, including Egyptian mummies.",
  "Dogs were domesticated thousands of years before cats, which only joined people once farming created stores of grain.",
  "The Cyprus grave with a cat was uncovered at the Shillourokambos site and reported by French archaeologists in 2004.",
  "The 4th-century Roman writer Palladius recommended keeping cats to protect garden beds from moles.",
  "The 7th-century scholar Isidore of Seville wrote that the cat was called musio because it was the enemy of mice.",
  "In medieval England a tomcat was commonly called Gib, short for Gilbert, and in France he was called Tibert.",
  "The 9th-century Book of Kells works cats and mice into the decoration of its most famous page.",
  "The Lindisfarne Gospels, made around the year 700, have a long cat stretched along the margin of one page.",
  "Medieval English church seats called misericords often show carved cats, sometimes catching mice or playing a fiddle.",
  "The witch's familiar in the shape of a cat was mostly an English feature of witch trials, and rarer on the Continent.",
  "At Elizabeth I's coronation celebrations in 1559, an effigy of the Pope filled with live cats was reportedly burned.",
  "In London's Great Plague of 1665 the Lord Mayor ordered cats and dogs killed, a toll Defoe later put at 200,000 cats.",
  "Domestic cats reached Hawaii aboard European ships in the late 18th century, and the islands had no cats before that.",
  "Albrecht Durer's 1504 engraving of Adam and Eve places a cat at Eve's feet, with a mouse within its reach.",
  "Lorenzo Lotto's 1534 Annunciation shows a cat fleeing from the angel, one of the most famous cats in Italian painting.",
  "Dutch painters of the 1600s often slipped a cat into their kitchen scenes, where it is usually eyeing the food.",
  "William Hogarth's 1742 portrait The Graham Children includes a cat eyeing a caged bird.",
  "The 18th-century naturalist Buffon disliked cats and wrote that people kept them only out of necessity.",
  "The Swiss artist Gottfried Mind, who died in 1814, painted cats so well that he was nicknamed the Raphael of Cats.",
  "Edouard Manet's 1863 painting Olympia includes a black cat standing at the foot of the bed.",
  "The Paris cabaret Le Chat Noir opened in 1881, and Steinlen's black cat poster for it became a famous cat image.",
  "Harrison Weir's 1889 book Our Cats and All About Them was one of the first books devoted to cat breeds and care.",
  "Harrison Weir said he organized the 1871 Crystal Palace show partly to improve the lot of cats, then widely despised.",
  "Louis Wain's breakthrough came in 1886 with a drawing of a kittens' Christmas party for the Illustrated London News.",
  "The manuscript that holds the poem Pangur Ban is kept today at a monastery in Austria.",
  "The Scottish Clan Chattan takes a cat as its crest, with the motto Touch not the cat but a glove.",
  "Two cat statues in St Petersburg honor the cats brought in to clear the city of rats after the siege of Leningrad.",
  "The first cloned cat was born in Texas in 2001, a few years after the cloning of a sheep made world headlines.",
  "Queen Victoria's fondness for cats helped make them fashionable pets in Victorian Britain.",
  "In the First World War soldiers adopted stray cats as mascots, and photographs show them in the trenches and dugouts.",
  "Cats went to sea with the Royal Navy in both world wars, and many ships listed a cat among their crew.",
  "Cats were kept in grain stores and warehouses of the ancient world long before anyone thought of them as pets.",
  "The Egyptians were among the first people to keep cats purely as companions, as their tomb art shows.",
  "Greek and Roman writers rarely mention cats as pets, a sign of how slowly cats caught on outside Egypt.",
  "Cats spread along Roman trade routes as far as Britain and the Rhine frontier by the 1st century AD.",
  "In the early Middle Ages cats were valued working animals on farms and in monasteries rather than pampered pets.",
  "Medieval European towns and farms relied on cats to protect stored grain, long before cats were kept for company.",
  "By the late Middle Ages the suspicion of cats as devilish had grown, though most households still kept them as mousers.",
  "In the 17th and 18th centuries cats slowly regained favor in Europe, appearing in portraits and in fashionable homes.",
  "By the 19th century the cat had become a popular pet in Europe and North America, kept for company as well as mousing.",
  "Cat shows, cat clubs and cat art in the late 19th century mark the moment the cat became a fashionable pet.",
  "The 20th century brought litter, commercial cat food and routine veterinary care, which made indoor cats common.",
  "In the 21st century cats are among the most numerous pets on Earth, found in homes on every inhabited continent.",
}

