  "Edward Lear's poem The Owl and the Pussy-Cat, in which the pair sail off in a pea-green boat, appeared in 1871.",
  "In Saki's story Tobermory (1911), a cat is taught to speak and promptly repeats the guests' secrets.",
  "The Cat Who Went to Heaven by Elizabeth Coatsworth won the Newbery Medal in 1931.",
  "Millions of Cats by Wanda Gag (1928) is often called the oldest American picture book still in print.",
  "In Paul Gallico's novel Jennie (1950), a boy turns into a cat and is taught how to live as one.",
  "Annie M. G. Schmidt's Minoes (1970), about a cat turned woman, is The Cat Who Came In Off the Roof in English.",
  "Thomas Gray's Ode on the Death of a Favourite Cat Drowned in a Tub of Goldfishes was written in 1747.",
  "W. B. Yeats's poem The Cat and the Moon follows a cat called Minnaloushe dancing under the changing moon.",
  "The nursery rhyme about a pussy cat who went to London to look at the Queen was first printed in the early 1800s.",
  "Hey Diddle Diddle, with its cat and fiddle, was printed in London in the 1760s.",
  "The rhyme Ding Dong Bell, about a cat down a well, is recorded from the 1580s.",
  "The fable of belling the cat, in which mice plan to hang a bell on their enemy, is at least 800 years old.",
  "In Aesop's fable of Venus and the Cat, a cat turned into a woman leaps from her bed to chase a mouse.",
  "In the Grimms' Bremen Town Musicians, a cat joins a donkey, a dog and a rooster to scare off robbers.",
  "In Collodi's Pinocchio (1883), the puppet is swindled by a pair called the Fox and the Cat.",
  "Haruki Murakami's novels are full of cats. In Kafka on the Shore an old man can talk to them.",
  "The nameless black cat in Neil Gaiman's Coraline can speak only in the Other World.",
  "The Cat That Lived a Million Times (1977) by Yoko Sano is one of Japan's best-loved picture books.",
  "In Ursula K. Le Guin's Catwings (1988), a litter of kittens is born with wings.",
  "Kurt Vonnegut's Cat's Cradle (1963) is named after the string game. One character notes there is no cat and no cradle.",
  "Margaret Atwood's novel Cat's Eye (1988) takes its title from a type of marble, not from a cat.",
  "Church, the cat in Stephen King's Pet Sematary (1983), is short for Winston Churchill.",
  "The Siamese cat Tao in Sheila Burnford's The Incredible Journey (1961) became Sassy in the film Homeward Bound (1993).",
  "Lying Cat, in the comic Saga, is a large hairless cat who announces Lying whenever someone tells a lie.",
  "Felix the Cat first appeared in 1919 in the short Feline Follies, making him one of the oldest cartoon stars.",
